Request one revision of Design before Build. Carry the replacement and recovery contract into the candidate and subsequent human interaction review, including cancellation, restoration, preview invalidation and complete handoff clearing. Teacher, student and caregiver review, any actual pilot and evidence of learning or transfer remain pending.","artifact_title":"Scenario draft: suggested wording displaces a qualified claim","challenged_assumption":"A learner's request to insert suggested wording sufficiently establishes intent to replace an existing claim and preserves recoverable qualifications.","decision":"Request a Design revision specifying occupied-field replacement, within-group restoration and complete clearing of recovery copies before Build.","drawbacks":["Replacement confirmation adds an interaction.","Temporary recovery introduces another copy to clear.","Additional controls may increase reading burden."],"evidence_refs":[15,22,24],"flourishing":["Agency and dignity: preserve qualifications and dissent through reversible replacement.","Access and belonging: use brief controls and retain oral, drawing and paper expression.","Sustainable workload and learning/transfer: review added effort; restored wording does not establish understanding."],"human_capabilities":["Learners need to inspect suggestions, decline replacement and restore their wording.","Teachers need a brief correction script and a paper-only route.","Reviewers need to examine replacement, recovery and handoff without treating completion as learning."],"message":"Hypothetical design challenge: a learner has written a qualified claim, then activates “insert suggested wording” while exploring choices. If insertion replaces the draft, their uncertainty disappears. Sequence 24 requires insertion on request but does not specify replacement safeguards. Revise Design before Build; this is a scenario expert draft, not an observed defect or approved requirement.","objections":["Hypothetical learner objection: “My uncertainty disappeared when I tried a suggested claim.”","Possible dissent: a teacher may prefer handwritten claims to another confirmation step.","Unknown: whether replacement safeguards are understandable across classroom languages and access routes."],"prototype_html":"","revisit_stage":"design","risks":["Lost qualifications could make a claim appear more confident.","Suggested wording could displace dissent.","An uncleared recovery copy could expose the previous group's writing."],"system_capabilities":["Insert directly only into an empty target; otherwise preview the suggestion and offer Replace or Cancel.","Provide explicit restoration of the immediately preceding target wording within the current group; invalidate stale discussion previews.","Confirmed handoff clearing must remove recovery copies alongside entries and previews."],"tradeoffs":["Fast insertion versus deliberate replacement of existing wording.","Within-group recovery versus complete removal at handoff.","Explicit confirmation versus added facilitation workload."]},"seq":27,"stage":"design"},{"agent":"orchestrator","response":{"advantages":["Inspectable suggestions and reversible replacement protect qualifications and dissent.","Literal, correctable previews support discussion without judging reasoning.","Prepared paper materials provide a complete non-AI route."],"artifact_body":"Status and basis
This is a rehearsal Design revision, not an approved classroom requirement. Sequence 24 integrates reasoning, participation, supported practice and visibility requirements; sequence 22 bounds delegation and disclosure. Sequence 27 is a hypothetical replacement event, not a technical finding or participant observation. No human approval, measured outcomes or demonstrated proficiency exist. All field evidence remains pending.

Revision responding to 27
Before: sequence 24 permits requested insertion of suggested claims but leaves occupied-field behavior unspecified. After: suggestions remain inspectable without changing writing. Insert directly only into an empty target. For occupied targets, identify the field, display current and proposed wording, and offer Replace and Cancel. Cancel preserves entries, selections, evidence visibility, recovery state and any valid preview. Design is the earliest affected artifact; this revision resolves that request without revisiting Understand or Model.

Evidence and layout
Use one responsive page: evidence, editable reasoning, then preview. Keep evidence accessible during editing. Use labelled native controls, visible focus, keyboard operation and information independent of color; accessibility remains unverified.
Each card states: “Invented classroom story: these numbers are story inputs, not study results.” Fictional period: 14 days. Water uses arbitrary story units per day, not plant-care advice.
A: water 1; bright light; final height 12 cm; upright leaves.
B: water 2; dim light; final height 18 cm; drooping leaves.
C: water 2; bright light; final height 10 cm; upright leaves.
Always disclose missing starting heights, plant type, soil and other conditions. Final height is not an increase; leaf descriptions do not establish health. “Better” requires a criterion.

Interaction and replacement contract
Begin with A/B. Offer “B is taller at the end,” “Taller means better,” “I cannot decide yet,” and original wording. An insertion action explicitly targets initial wording or current claim. Suggestions never imply correctness. Ask what differs in observations and recorded conditions; A/B differ in water and light.
Reveal C on request as additional evidence. Hiding or reopening C preserves writing. Invite A/C and B/C comparison without implying that recorded similarities isolate causation.
Maintain separately editable initial wording, current claim, comparison, support, uncertainty and optional dissent. Retain, narrow, revise and withhold are optional; neither text nor changed wording is required. Initial wording is working text, not an authenticated first thought.
Confirmed replacement retains only the immediately preceding wording for that target in temporary recovery memory. Offer Restore previous wording, showing its target and saved text. If restoring would displace subsequent edits, show current and saved wording with Restore and Cancel. Restoration is deliberate, consumes that recovery copy and never occurs automatically. Further replacement updates only that target's recovery copy. Replacement, restoration or editing invalidates the preview. Pending confirmation must resolve before another text-changing action.

Output, visibility and handoff
A requested preview assembles literal current entries, reconsideration choice and fixed missing-information text. Blanks say “Text not supplied.” Representation defaults to unresolved; one or several explanations are user descriptions, never verified agreement.
Beside entries: “People nearby can see this screen. Use fictional evidence; do not enter names or personal details. You may discuss, draw or use paper without entering text.” Beside preview: “Working wording for discussion; it does not verify contribution, agreement or learning. Correct it or leave disagreement unresolved.”
Clear for next group explains removal and offers Cancel and Clear. Cancel preserves state. Confirmed Clear removes entries, selections, preview, pending replacements and all recovery copies, resets C and announces clearing. No restoration crosses handoff or survives refresh. Clearing cannot retract copies or what others saw. Use no identities, participant notes, storage, telemetry, external resources or network calls. No grades, rankings, inferred disability or competence judgments.

Paper route, practice and gates
Copy A/B/C onto separate sheets; hold C until requested. Provide optional fields matching the browser entries and representation prompt. Put suggested claims on a separate sheet so inspection preserves original wording. Allow oral explanation, drawing and supported recording; expose recorder wording for correction. Passing preserves evidence access and later contribution. Continue on paper after device failure without mandatory re-entry. Paper also has visibility limits; route equivalence is unknown.
Ask “Which observation supports your wording?” Optional practice: “Choose another pair. What can you claim, and what remains uncertain?” Keep earlier prompts available. This is supported practice, not proof of independence. Separate delayed or cross-context inquiry requires human review and stays outside the candidate.
Apparent lost uncertainty could reflect intentional replacement, recorder influence or spoken wording never entered. Sparse responses may reflect reading, typing, time or pressure; fluent responses may reflect copying. Plant differences may involve water, light, starting height or missing conditions.
Build next. Before real use, require teacher, student and caregiver review and human interaction testing of insertion, Cancel, replacement, restoration after edits, preview invalidation, reveal, keyboard access, complete clearing and paper continuation. Examine workload and participation separately from learning.","artifact_title":"Revised Design: fictional evidence, deliberate replacement and recovery","challenged_assumption":"Requesting insertion establishes intent to replace existing wording and adequately preserves displaced qualifications.","decision":"Revise Design sequence 24 in response to hypothetical sequence 27 using this replacement and recovery contract, then advance to Build within the rehearsal. Real use and any inquiry remain subject to pending human review and evidence.","drawbacks":["Confirmation and restoration add reading and interaction demands.","Shared screens expose wording and disagreement.","Facilitation, paper preparation and separate learning inquiry require teacher time."],"evidence_refs":[22,24,27],"flourishing":["Agency and dignity: preserve qualifications, correction and the right to decline recording without justification.","Access and belonging: retain evidence access, oral explanation, drawing and paper participation while preserving dissent.","Sustainable workload and learning/transfer: supply reusable materials and investigate reasoning separately from completion."],"human_capabilities":["Learners need opportunities to inspect, retain, withhold, correct and dissent through chosen participation routes.","Teachers need prepared materials and time to expose recorder wording for correction and permit paper-only participation.","Teacher, student and caregiver reviewers need to examine control comprehension, reading burden, visibility and workload before real use."],"message":"Sequence 27 describes a hypothetical loss of qualified wording, not an observed defect. It exposes an unspecified replacement behavior in Design sequence 24. This revision adopts explicit replacement, cancellation and within-group restoration, extending complete handoff clearing to recovery copies. Design is the earliest affected artifact; Understand and Model need no revision. Proceed to Build in the rehearsal using this revised contract. Human review, interaction testing and field evidence remain pending.","objections":["Hypothetical learner dissent: “Trying a suggestion should not erase my uncertainty.”","Hypothetical teacher dissent: paper-only discussion may avoid excessive control and device-management demands.","Unknown: whether classroom languages, reading burden, device access and discussion norms support these arrangements."],"prototype_html":"","revisit_stage":"design","risks":["Replacement or restoration could erase qualifications if its target is unclear.","Uncleared recovery copies could expose a previous group's wording.","Completion, fluent summaries or representation labels could become unsupported assessment proxies."],"system_capabilities":["Show fictional evidence and missing conditions; preserve editable explanations without scoring or inferring agreement.","Identify insertion targets; confirm occupied-field replacement and offer deliberate within-group restoration.","Invalidate obsolete previews and clear entries, pending actions and recovery copies at confirmed handoff; use no storage or network."],"tradeoffs":["Fast insertion into empty fields versus deliberate replacement of existing wording.","Within-group recovery versus complete removal at handoff.","Structured prompts ease entry but may supply the reasoning being elicited."]},"seq":29,"stage":"design"},{"agent":"implementation","response":{"advantages":["A small deterministic interface can expose wording changes without evaluating learners.","Explicit state transitions make cancellation and recovery reviewable.","Prepared paper materials support continuation without re-entering lost writing."],"artifact_body":"Status and basis
This is a proposed implementation contract, not an approved requirement or tested artifact. Sequence 24 supplies the workbench design; sequence 27 supplies a hypothetical replacement event; sequence 29 adopts deliberate replacement and recovery. Sequence 22 bounds disclosure and delegation. No participant observations, approvals, measured results or demonstrated proficiency exist. Field evidence remains pending. This specialist turn supplies no executable prototype.

Implementation challenge and proposed Build change
Before: treating empty visible fields as sufficient evidence of complete handoff clearing.
After: define and inspect every application-owned copy: editable entries, preview nodes, pending proposal text, per-target recovery text and any temporary rendering references. Confirmed handoff must remove all of them. Recreate editable controls at handoff rather than relying only on assigning empty values, then investigate browser undo and autofill using fictional text. Do not describe this as secure erasure: browser-managed state, copies and what others saw remain outside that assurance. This makes sequence 29 concrete without requesting another Design revision.

Small candidate scope
Use one self-contained page with inline CSS and JavaScript and native labelled controls. Show fictional A/B initially and C on request: A—water 1, bright light, final height 12 cm, upright leaves; B—water 2, dim light, 18 cm, drooping leaves; C—water 2, bright light, 10 cm, upright leaves. Each card carries the invented-story label. State the fictional 14-day period, arbitrary water units and missing starting heights, plant type, soil and other conditions. Final height is not an increase; leaf wording does not establish health.
Maintain separate optional initial wording, current claim, comparison, support, uncertainty and dissent entries. Reconsideration and representation remain user descriptions, never judgments. Assemble requested output from literal entries using textContent, with blanks shown as “Text not supplied.” Include fixed limitations beside the output. No evaluation, compulsory revision or consequential score belongs in the candidate.

Transition contract
Use editing, replacement confirmation, restoration confirmation and clear confirmation states. While confirmation is pending, block other text-changing actions and freeze affected selections. Show the target, current wording and proposed wording; retain only the minimum pending copy. Cancel returns to editing without altering entries, selections, reveal state, recovery or a valid preview.
Insert a suggestion directly only into an empty target. Confirmed occupied-field replacement saves that target's immediately preceding wording. Further replacement updates only that target's recovery copy. Show the saved wording before deliberate restoration; confirm any displacement of subsequent edits. Restoration consumes its recovery copy. Editing, replacement, restoration and output-relevant selection changes remove the old preview; evidence reveal alone preserves it.
Confirmed clearing resets entries, selections, C visibility, preview, pending actions and all recovery copies. Announce clearing and provide no application undo across handoff. Opening or canceling clear must preserve state. Handle Escape as Cancel and return keyboard focus to the initiating control. These are proposed behaviors, not verified accessibility.

Review instrumentation without participant records
Use a manual reviewer worksheet outside the candidate with synthetic text, expected behavior, observed behavior and unresolved defects. Do not collect interaction counts, timestamps, task notes or learner traces in the browser.
Pending checks: replace occupied wording and Cancel; replace, edit again, then restore or Cancel; replace each target independently; edit after preview; hide/reopen C; cancel and confirm handoff; attempt restoration and browser undo after clearing; operate controls by keyboard. Inspect output with markup-like fictional text to ensure literal rendering. No checks have been run here. Passing technical checks would establish only those tested behaviors.

Prepared non-AI fallback
Copy the labelled A/B/C cards above onto separate sheets and hold C until requested. Provide optional matching response fields and separate suggested-claim slips. Learners may discuss, draw or use supported recording; expose recorder wording for correction. Continue on paper after failure without mandatory re-entry. Paper-only delivery remains legitimate; paper visibility and route equivalence are unresolved.

Human gates
Before real use, require teacher, student and caregiver review plus human interaction testing. Examine reading burden, device access, discussion norms and teacher workload separately from scientific reasoning. Completion cannot establish learning or transfer; those require separately reviewed inquiry. No launch or pilot is authorized by this draft.","artifact_title":"Build expert draft: atomic correction, handoff checks and paper fallback","challenged_assumption":"Clearing visible fields sufficiently establishes that all recoverable wording has been removed at shared-device handoff.","decision":"Carry this implementation contract into the Build synthesis and its small interactive candidate. Review synthetic correction and handoff behavior before considering real use; preserve paper-only delivery and pending human gates.","drawbacks":["Confirmation and recovery controls increase reading and interaction demands.","Temporary writing is lost on refresh or device failure.","Shared screens and browser behavior limit privacy assurances."],"evidence_refs":[22,24,27,29],"flourishing":["Agency and dignity: make replacement deliberate and allow withholding or correction without justification.","Access and belonging: retain evidence access, dissent and oral, drawing or paper participation.","Sustainable workload and learning/transfer: provide reusable materials and investigate reasoning separately from interaction completion."],"human_capabilities":["Learners need opportunities to inspect, correct, decline recording and preserve disagreement.","Teachers need prepared paper materials and time to check recorder wording and switch routes.","Reviewers need to examine interaction behavior, reading burden, device access and discussion norms before real use."],"message":"Sequence 29 resolves the hypothetical replacement challenge in sequence 27 at the specification level. Build must now make cancellation, recovery and handoff clearing consistent across every interaction. My implementation challenge is that clearing visible fields alone may leave an obsolete preview, recovery copy or browser-managed edit history. This is an expert draft for a rehearsal; no candidate has been executed or tested in this turn. Human review and all field evidence remain pending.","objections":["Hypothetical teacher dissent: paper-only discussion may justify less management than recovery controls.","Rival explanations: missing uncertainty may reflect intentional replacement, recorder influence or spoken wording never entered; sparse text may reflect reading or typing demands.","Unknowns: browser undo behavior, control comprehension, language access and acceptable facilitation time remain untested."],"prototype_html":"","revisit_stage":"","risks":["A stale preview could misrepresent corrected uncertainty or dissent.","Incomplete clearing could expose previous wording to the next group.","Successful interaction checks could be mistaken for accessibility or learning evidence."],"system_capabilities":["Implement explicit confirmation states with literal previews and no automatic judgments.","Preserve cancellation state, invalidate stale previews and consume restored recovery copies.","Clear application-owned entries and copies at handoff; use no storage, telemetry, identities or network."],"tradeoffs":["Recovery preserves qualifications within a group but creates copies that handoff must remove.","Blocking edits during confirmation protects consistency but interrupts discussion.","Manual synthetic checks avoid participant telemetry but require reviewer time."]},"seq":35,"stage":"build"},{"agent":"orchestrator","response":{"advantages":["Visible fictional evidence and editable uncertainty support reviewable discussion.","Deliberate replacement and recovery preserve opportunities to correct qualifications.","Matching paper prompts support a complete non-AI route."],"artifact_body":"Status and basis
This is an executable candidate for a rehearsal, not an approved requirement or certified solution. Sequence 20 separates supported practice from transfer; 22 bounds visibility and delegation; 27 supplies a hypothetical replacement challenge; 29 revises Design; 35 specifies implementation transitions and handoff inspection. No real participant observations, authorization, measured results or demonstrated proficiency exist. Field evidence remains pending.

Build scope
The accompanying self-contained HTML displays A/B and reveals C on request. Every card labels its numbers as invented story inputs. The fictional period is 14 days; water uses arbitrary story units, not plant-care guidance. A: water 1, bright light, final height 12 cm, upright leaves. B: water 2, dim light, final height 18 cm, drooping leaves. C: water 2, bright light, final height 10 cm, upright leaves. Missing starting heights, plant type, soil and other conditions limit interpretation. Final height is not increase; leaf wording does not establish health; “better” needs a criterion.

Meaningful changes after challenges
Sequence 27 challenged unspecified insertion into occupied fields. Following 29, selecting a suggestion changes no writing; insertion names its target. Empty targets accept insertion directly. Occupied targets show current and proposed wording with Replace and Cancel. Replacement saves the immediately preceding wording for that target. Restoration shows both versions, requires confirmation and consumes its recovery copy. Independent initial/current recovery is supported. This is implemented behavior awaiting execution, not a verified benefit.
Sequence 35 challenges visible-field clearing as sufficient handoff assurance. Confirmed Clear recreates editable controls, resets selections and C, removes preview and dialog text, and discards pending and recovery objects. This addresses application-owned copies. It makes no secure-erasure claim about browser-managed undo, autofill, copies or what others saw. No earlier artifact needs another revision.

Interaction and output contract
Initial wording, current claim, comparison, support, uncertainty and dissent remain separately editable and optional. Retain, narrow, revise and withhold are descriptions, not required outcomes. Representation defaults to unresolved and never verifies agreement. Preview renders literal current entries and fixed limitations; blanks say “Text not supplied.” Editing or output-relevant selection changes remove the old preview. Evidence reveal alone preserves it. Native modal confirmation blocks other interactions; Cancel or Escape preserves entries, selections, evidence visibility, recovery and a valid preview. Focus returns to the initiating control. None of these behaviors has been tested here.

Human responsibilities and paper packet
A facilitator asks whose explanation is represented, exposes recorder wording for correction and preserves dissent. Roles are optional; passing preserves evidence access and later contribution. Learners may explain orally, draw, point with explanation or use supported recording without browser entry. Do not use browser wording as individual assessment evidence.
Paper-only delivery is a complete non-AI option. Copy the three labelled cards onto separate sheets and hold C until requested. Use optional response spaces: initial wording; current claim; compared pair and changed conditions; supporting observation; uncertainty; alternative wording/dissent; reconsideration; representation. Put suggested claims on separate slips. Ask “Which observation supports your wording?” Optional practice: “Choose another pair. What can you claim, and what remains uncertain?” Earlier prompts stay available. Continue on paper after device failure without mandatory re-entry. Paper visibility and route equivalence remain unresolved.

Review plan and next action
Proceed to synthetic interaction review in the rehearsal. Outside the candidate, use fictional wording to record expected behavior, observed behavior and unresolved defects. Check occupied insertion and Cancel; independent target recovery; replacement followed by typing and restoration/Cancel; preview invalidation; hide/reopen C; Escape and keyboard focus; clear cancellation and confirmed handoff; attempted recovery and browser undo afterward; literal rendering of markup-like text. Check small-screen reading and paper continuation. No checks have been run.
Before any real use, teacher, student and caregiver review and human interaction testing remain required. Investigate reading burden, language access, device availability, discussion norms and teacher effort separately from learning. Apparent lost uncertainty may reflect intentional replacement, recorder influence or spoken wording never entered. Plant differences could involve water, light, starting height or missing conditions. Completion and polished summaries do not establish reasoning, retention or transfer; separate inquiry requires human review. No pilot, launch or efficacy decision is made.","artifact_title":"Build candidate: fictional evidence, correction and shared-device handoff","challenged_assumption":"Deliberate replacement and clearing visible fields alone sufficiently preserve qualifications and remove all recoverable application wording at handoff.","decision":"Advance to Instrument within the rehearsal for synthetic interaction checks and preparation of human review materials. The candidate remains unexecuted; real use, any pilot and learning inquiry remain behind pending human review and evidence.","drawbacks":["Choices and prompts may supply reasoning rather than reveal independent capability.","Confirmation controls add reading and facilitation demands.","Shared screens expose wording; temporary work disappears on refresh."],"evidence_refs":[20,22,27,29,35],"flourishing":["Agency and dignity: permit correction, withholding and declining browser recording without explanation.","Access and belonging: retain evidence access and oral, drawing, paper and supported-recording routes with unresolved dissent.","Sustainable workload and learning/transfer: provide reusable prompts and investigate reasoning separately from completion."],"human_capabilities":["Learners need opportunities to inspect, pass, correct, withhold and dissent through chosen modes.","Teachers need prepared paper materials and time to expose recorder wording, facilitate competing explanations and switch routes.","Teacher, student and caregiver reviewers need accessible ways to challenge reading burden, visibility, participation and workload."],"message":"Build integrates revised Design (29) and the implementation challenge (35). Sequence 27 was a hypothetical loss of qualified wording, not an observed defect; the candidate implements deliberate replacement and recovery. Clearing now recreates entry controls and removes application-owned previews, proposals and recovery copies. No execution, interaction testing, human approval or field evidence exists. Proceed to synthetic interaction review and preparation for human review within the rehearsal.","objections":["Hypothetical learner dissent: “Trying a suggestion should not erase my uncertainty.” Recovery remains deliberate and inspectable.","Hypothetical teacher dissent: paper-only discussion may leave more time for reasoning than device management.","Rivals and unknowns: sparse writing may reflect reading, typing or pressure; fluent wording may reflect copying or recorder influence. Languages, device access and discussion norms remain unknown."],"prototype_html":"<!doctype html><html lang="en"><head><meta charset="utf-8"><meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width,initial-scale=1"><title>Fictional evidence workbench</title><style>body{font:17px/1.5 system-ui,sans-serif;max-width:960px;margin:auto;padding:20px;color:#172638;background:#f7f8fa}h1,h2,h3{line-height:1.2}section,article,details{background:white;border:1px solid #aab5c0;border-radius:8px;padding:14px;margin:14px 0}.cards{display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:12px}.cards article{flex:1;min-width:190px}label{display:block;margin-top:12px;font-weight:600}textarea,select,button{font:inherit}textarea{display:block;box-sizing:border-box;width:100%;min-height:76px}select{max-width:100%}button{padding:8px 12px;margin:8px 6px 8px 0;cursor:pointer}button:disabled{cursor:default}button:focus-visible,textarea:focus-visible,select:focus-visible,summary:focus-visible{outline:3px solid #185ca0;outline-offset:3px}.notice{background:#edf3f8;padding:12px}.small{font-size:.9em}pre{white-space:pre-wrap;overflow-wrap:anywhere;font:inherit;margin:6px 0}dialog{max-width:650px;width:calc(100% - 70px);border:2px solid #34495e;border-radius:8px}dialog::backdrop{background:#0008}[hidden]{display:none!important}</style></head><body><h1>Reasoning when evidence conflicts</h1><p class="notice">Rehearsal candidate requiring human review and interaction testing. No learning results or approvals exist. No grades, rankings or automatic judgments.</p><section><h2>1. Inspect fictional evidence</h2><p>Fictional period: 14 days. Water amounts are arbitrary story units per day, not plant-care advice.</p><div class="cards"><article><h3>A</h3><p class="small">Invented classroom story: these numbers are story inputs, not study results.</p><p>Water: 1<br>Light: bright<br>Final height: 12 cm<br>Leaves: upright</p></article><article><h3>B</h3><p class="small">Invented classroom story: these numbers are story inputs, not study results.</p><p>Water: 2<br>Light: dim<br>Final height: 18 cm<br>Leaves: drooping</p></article><article id="cardC" hidden><h3>C — additional evidence</h3><p class="small">Invented classroom story: these numbers are story inputs, not study results.</p><p>Water: 2<br>Light: bright<br>Final height: 10 cm<br>Leaves: upright</p></article></div><button id="reveal" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="cardC">Reveal C: compare with another observation</button><p id="missing">Starting heights, plant type, soil and other conditions are unrecorded. Final height is not an increase. Leaf descriptions do not establish health. “Better” needs a criterion. These cards do not isolate causes.</p><details><summary>Optional comparison hint</summary><p>A/B differ in recorded water and light. With C visible, compare A/C and B/C. Similar recorded conditions do not establish equal unrecorded conditions. C challenges “more water always accompanies greater final height”; it does not refute every claim.</p></details></section><section><h2>2. Explain or discuss</h2><p class="notice">People nearby can see this screen. Use fictional evidence; do not enter names or personal details. You may discuss, draw or use paper without entering text. Passing preserves access and a later contribution opportunity.</p><p>What differs in observations and recorded conditions? Which observation supports your wording? All entries are optional. Initial wording is editable working text, not an authenticated first thought.</p><div id="work"><label for="suggestion">Optional suggested wording — inspecting it changes no entry</label><select id="suggestion"><option value="">Use my own wording in the fields below</option><option>B is taller at the end.</option><option>Taller means better.</option><option>I cannot decide yet.</option></select><label for="target">Insert suggestion into</label><select id="target"><option value="initial">Initial wording</option><option value="current">Current claim</option></select><button id="insert">Insert selected wording</button><p class="small">Suggestions are prompts, not verified answers. Occupied fields require confirmation.</p><label for="initial">Initial wording</label><textarea id="initial" autocomplete="off"></textarea><label for="current">Current claim — retain or change it as you choose</label><textarea id="current" autocomplete="off"></textarea><label for="comparison">Pair compared and changed recorded conditions</label><textarea id="comparison" autocomplete="off"></textarea><label for="support">Supporting observation</label><textarea id="support" autocomplete="off"></textarea><label for="uncertainty">Uncertainty or information needed</label><textarea id="uncertainty" autocomplete="off"></textarea><label for="dissent">Optional alternative wording or dissent</label><textarea id="dissent" autocomplete="off"></textarea><label for="reconsider">Reconsideration description</label><select id="reconsider"><option>Not selected</option><option>Retain</option><option>Narrow</option><option>Revise</option><option>Withhold</option></select><label for="representation">Whose explanation does this represent? No names.</label><select id="representation"><option>Representation unresolved</option><option>One explanation</option><option>Several explanations</option></select><p>These descriptions do not verify agreement. Invite correction and preserve disagreement.</p><h3>Temporary recovery within this group</h3><p>Only the preceding replaced wording for each target is retained. Restoration requires confirmation and consumes its copy.</p><button id="restoreInitial" disabled>Restore previous initial wording</button><pre id="savedInitial"></pre><button id="restoreCurrent" disabled>Restore previous current claim</button><pre id="savedCurrent"></pre></div><p>Optional practice: Choose another pair. What can you claim, and what remains uncertain? Earlier prompts remain available. This is supported practice, not evidence of independence or transfer.</p><button id="preview">Preview current discussion wording</button></section><section><h2>3. Correctable discussion preview</h2><p>Working wording for discussion; it does not verify contribution, agreement or learning. Correct the fields above or leave disagreement unresolved. Completion is not evidence of scientific reasoning.</p><div id="output" hidden></div></section><section><h2>Paper-only alternative</h2><p>A teacher can copy A/B/C onto separate sheets; reveal C here to prepare its sheet, then hold that sheet until requested in the paper activity. Every sheet keeps the invented-story label and missing-information notice. Put suggested claims on separate slips.</p><p>Optional response sheet: Initial wording; Current claim; Pair and changed conditions; Supporting observation; Uncertainty; Alternative wording/dissent; Retain/narrow/revise/withhold; Whose explanation is represented?</p><p>Discuss orally, draw, point with explanation or use supported recording. Make recorder wording available for correction. Continue on paper after device failure without mandatory re-entry. Paper also has visibility limits; equivalence between routes is unknown.</p></section><section><h2>Shared-device handoff</h2><p>Refresh or closing loses temporary writing. Clear removes application-owned entries, previews, pending proposals and recovery copies. It cannot retract copies or what others saw. Browser-managed undo and autofill remain untested; this is not secure erasure.</p><button id="clear">Clear for next group…</button></section><p id="status" role="status" aria-live="polite"></p><dialog id="confirm" aria-labelledby="dialogTitle"><h2 id="dialogTitle"></h2><pre id="dialogText"></pre><button id="accept">Confirm</button><button id="cancel">Cancel</button></dialog><script>/* Candidate only. No storage, telemetry, network or evaluation. Human interaction checks remain pending. */
const $=id=>document.getElementById(id),work=$('work'),template=work.innerHTML,dlg=$('confirm');
const fields=['initial','current','comparison','support','uncertainty','dissent'];
const labels={initial:'Initial wording',current:'Current claim',comparison:'Pair and changed conditions',support:'Supporting observation',uncertainty:'Uncertainty',dissent:'Alternative wording / dissent'};
let recovery={},pending=null,origin=null;
function say(s){$('status').textContent=s}
function invalidate(){const out=$('output');out.replaceChildren();out.hidden=true;say('Wording changed. Request a new preview when ready.')}
function recoverView(){for(const [id,suffix] of [['initial','Initial'],['current','Current']]){$('restore'+suffix).disabled=!(id in recovery);$('saved'+suffix).textContent=id in recovery?'Saved '+labels[id]+': '+recovery[id]:''}}
function ask(action,title,text,button,source){pending=action;origin=source;$('dialogTitle').textContent=title;$('dialogText').textContent=text;$('accept').textContent=button;dlg.showModal();$('cancel').focus()}
function finish(){pending=null;$('dialogTitle').textContent='';$('dialogText').textContent='';dlg.close();const focus=origin;origin=null;if(focus&&focus.isConnected)focus.focus()}
$('cancel').onclick=()=>finish();dlg.addEventListener('cancel',e=>{e.preventDefault();finish()});
work.addEventListener('input',e=>{if(e.target.tagName==='TEXTAREA')invalidate()});
work.addEventListener('change',e=>{if(['reconsider','representation'].includes(e.target.id))invalidate()});
