This is an expert draft for the rehearsal, not an approved requirement or inquiry protocol. Sequence 20 separates supported practice from transfer; 22 excludes participant notes from the candidate; 35 proposes synthetic review; 37 supplies an unexecuted Build candidate. No participant observations, approvals, measured results or demonstrated proficiency exist. Field evidence remains pending.

Measurement challenge and requirement change
Before: sequence 37 names interaction checks and separate learning inquiry but leaves evidence interpretation and comparison procedures unspecified.
After: require separate technical, reasoning, participation and workload records. A technical pass supports only the tested behavior. A discussion preview is editable recorded wording, not an authenticated baseline, individual assessment or evidence of agreement. Do not calculate improvement from its initial/current fields. Keep fixed missing-information text separate from learner-supplied explanations: displaying or copying that notice does not demonstrate understanding. This adds an Instrument requirement without reopening Build.

Synthetic reviewer worksheet
Use fictional text only, outside the candidate. One row per check: candidate revision; browser/viewport/input method; starting state; action; expected behavior; observed behavior; evidence location; discrepancy; unresolved question. Observed behavior and evidence location remain blank until execution. Classify outcomes as observed as expected, discrepancy or untested; these are technical descriptions, not learner scores.
Probe occupied insertion followed by Cancel; independent recovery for each target; restoration after further editing; preview invalidation; reveal/hide preservation; Escape and focus return; clear cancellation; confirmed clearing followed by attempted recovery and browser undo; literal markup-like text. Record application behavior separately from browser-managed history. No check has been run and no secure-erasure assurance follows.

Draft reasoning lenses for later human review
Seek an explanation connecting a claim to a specific observation; identifying recorded similarities and differences; distinguishing final height from increase; and explaining how missing conditions limit causal conclusions. Reconsideration may retain, narrow, revise or withhold with reasons. Treat each lens as evidenced in this task, ambiguous, not elicited or not observed, with a brief supporting example if a separately approved inquiry permits collection. No totals, grades, rankings or proficiency labels.
Accept speech, drawing, pointing with explanation, writing and supported recording. An empty browser field means text not supplied. A group explanation cannot establish individual capability. Correction, dissent and requests for support remain valid actions rather than failures.

Comparison proposal, not an authorized study
Use teacher-facilitated paper discussion as the complete non-AI comparison option. Match fictional evidence, available time, facilitator prompts and access supports as far as practicable; document differences rather than asserting route equivalence. Compare reasoning opportunities, correction, participation and teacher effort separately.
Self-selected routes cannot isolate a device effect. If a later reviewed inquiry compares routes within learners or groups, propose counterbalanced order and distinct matched fictional tasks; repeated A/B/C exposure risks practice effects. Any assignment method requires review. Report assistance, exposure, missing observations and implementation differences alongside findings. Neither synthetic checks nor a before/after discussion licenses a causal efficacy claim.

Transfer and retention
Sequence 20's cart task repeats the plant story's numerical pattern, so success could reflect pattern matching. Draft a fresh fictional task with a different evidence structure: for example, a challenged causal claim with no direct counterexample, requiring withholding because conditions differ. Review its language and scientific interpretation before use. Begin with an open prompt; retain evidence visibility and access supports, and distinguish explanatory hints from language assistance. A later task at a reviewed delay addresses retention separately. Immediate supported performance establishes neither retention nor transfer.

Coverage, workload and gates
Prepare a synthetic coverage matrix crossing paper/shared-device routes, reading and language presentation, contribution modes and discussion arrangements. Mark each cell planned, checked or unknown; no cells are checked here. Later coverage must use only reviewed, voluntary information, never inferred disability or ability. No observed difference cannot establish equity when relevant coverage is absent.
Review preparation, facilitation, correction and recovery effort alongside opportunities to inspect, contribute and dissent. Preserve disagreement about whether instrumentation is worth its burden. Any real collection needs a separately reviewed purpose, minimum information, access, retention and correction process. Teacher, student and caregiver review and human interaction testing remain pending; no pilot or launch is authorized.","artifact_title":"Instrument expert draft: task evidence, comparison and coverage","challenged_assumption":"Successful interactions and polished discussion wording can support claims about reasoning, transfer or equitable access without a specified comparison and coverage method.","decision":"Continue Instrument by preparing the external synthetic worksheet and comparison/coverage drafts for review. Execute no checks or participant inquiry in this turn; retain all human gates and field evidence as pending.","drawbacks":["Manual review and separate transfer tasks require facilitator time.","Shared wording cannot establish each learner's reasoning.","Comparable tasks and participation routes remain unvalidated."],"evidence_refs":[20,22,35,37],"flourishing":["Agency and dignity: declining recording, retaining claims and dissent remain legitimate.","Access and belonging: investigate who can inspect, contribute and correct across routes.","Sustainable workload and learning/transfer: examine facilitation effort separately from reasoning and delayed performance."],"human_capabilities":["Reviewers must distinguish observed task behavior, interpretation and missing evidence.","Facilitators must preserve access supports, correction, dissent and optional participation.","Teacher, student and caregiver reviewers must examine any proposed inquiry before real use."],"message":"Sequence 37 proposes synthetic interaction review, but technical correctness cannot establish reasoning, equitable participation or transfer. Instrument should separate these evidence streams and specify a comparison method before any learning claim. This Measurement expert draft adds descriptive task lenses, comparison safeguards and coverage checks. No checks were executed; human review and all field evidence remain pending.","objections":["Hypothetical teacher dissent: paper-only discussion may leave more time for reasoning than instrument administration.","Rivals: apparent improvement may reflect cue familiarity, copying, recorder influence or teacher assistance.","Unknowns: language access, reading burden, device availability, discussion norms and task comparability."],"prototype_html":"","revisit_stage":"","risks":["Copied limitations or fluent recorder wording may resemble reasoning evidence.","Route selection, teacher assistance and prior familiarity can confound comparisons.","Absent subgroup evidence may be misreported as equitable access."],"system_capabilities":["Keep discussion previews separate from any external review worksheet; add no participant telemetry.","Expose evidence, optional hints and correction without scores or competence judgments.","Support synthetic transition checks and matching paper materials without personal records."],"tradeoffs":["Comparable prompts improve interpretation but can reproduce cueing.","Minimal collection protects dignity but limits subgroup conclusions.","Fresh transfer tasks reduce rehearsal effects but introduce unfamiliar vocabulary."]},"seq":42,"stage":"instrument"},{"agent":"orchestrator","response":{"advantages":["Separate evidence streams prevent technical checks from becoming learning claims.","Explicit expected behaviors make correction, cancellation and handoff reviewable.","Paper-only comparison preserves a complete non-AI option."],"artifact_body":"This is an integrated Instrument draft for a rehearsal, not an approved inquiry protocol. Sequence 37 supplies the unexecuted Build candidate; 35 specifies transition review; 42 adds separate evidence streams, comparison safeguards and coverage. Sequences 20 and 22 bound learning claims and collection. No real observations, measured results, approvals or demonstrated proficiency exist. Field evidence remains pending.

