Challenge and proposed Evaluate change
Before: 51 carries a review packet and learning cautions forward without specifying how Evaluate must adjudicate each proposed conclusion.
After: require an external claim ledger identifying the exact claim, relevant evidence stream, candidate revision, observation, source of wording, assistance, rival explanation, missing coverage and permissible conclusion. Distinguish absent evidence from observed difficulty and from behavior not elicited. No aggregate success judgment, score or proficiency label follows. This adds an Evaluate requirement; no supplied finding invalidates an earlier artifact.

Blank claim ledger
Claim under review: __. Candidate/task/context: __. Evidence needed: __. Observed behavior: pending. Evidence location: pending. Learner explanation versus supplied text: unresolved. Explanatory hints and access supports: __. Rival explanation: __. Missing observations or coverage: __. Narrow conclusion: unestablished. Unresolved dissent: __. Next review action: __.
Use fictional inputs for technical review. Any later participant record requires the separately reviewed purpose, minimum information, access, retention, correction and reuse arrangements in 51. Do not turn the synthetic worksheet into a learner log.

Evidence boundaries
Technical: outcomes from 44 remain untested. A future observed transition supports only that behavior in the tested revision, browser and input conditions. It cannot establish secure erasure, accessibility across settings or learning.
Reasoning: seek an explanation connecting a claim to an observation, comparing recorded conditions, distinguishing final height from increase and explaining causal uncertainty. Keep fixed notices and copied hints distinguishable from explanations. Retaining, narrowing, revising or withholding can each be reasoned. Editable initial/current text cannot establish improvement; group wording cannot establish individual capability. Blank text means text not supplied.
Participation and workload: examine opportunities to inspect evidence, contribute, correct recorder wording and dissent, alongside preparation, facilitation and recovery effort. Completion and silence do not establish access, agreement or acceptable workload.

Comparison and causal threats
Teacher-facilitated paper discussion using the labelled A/B/C cards and matching optional prompts is the complete non-AI option. Ask whether the browser contributes useful correction or evidence-inspection opportunities relative to its reading and management demands. Match evidence, time, prompts and supports where practicable; report actual differences.
Self-selected routes cannot identify a device effect. A later reviewed comparison could use distinct matched tasks and counterbalanced order, while documenting assistance and implementation differences. Carryover, prior familiarity, recorder influence, novelty, task difficulty and facilitator prompting remain rival explanations. Plant observations themselves cannot isolate water or light effects: starting height and other conditions are missing.

Transfer, coverage and unknowns
A fresh fictional task should change the evidence structure, rather than repeat the plant story's counterexample pattern. An open prompt with evidence and language supports available can examine reasoning in that task; immediate performance cannot establish retention. A separately reviewed delayed task is needed for any retention claim. Neither establishes broad transfer without further evidence.
Review coverage across paper/shared-device routes, reading and language presentation, oral/drawing/written contributions and discussion arrangements. Mark planned, observed and unknown coverage separately. No coverage is observed here. Do not infer disability or ability from route choice. If a relevant context is absent, withhold conclusions about it rather than treating missing complaints as equitable access.

Decision and human gates
Next, prepare the blank ledger alongside 44's synthetic worksheet and 51's decision record for review. Pending technical execution, human interaction testing and teacher, student and caregiver review must remain explicit. Any real inquiry requires separate human review; classroom use requires a revision-specific human decision. Preserve dissent that paper alone may better fit available discussion time. No pilot, launch, efficacy, equity or proficiency claim is supported by this rehearsal.","artifact_title":"Evaluate expert draft: evidence ledger and limits on conclusions","challenged_assumption":"A bounded review plan is sufficient to support evaluation conclusions without explicit claim-level evidence, missingness and coverage rules.","decision":"Continue Evaluate as a rehearsal by proposing the claim ledger and explicit missing-evidence rules for review. Keep technical execution, human gates and field inquiry pending; request no earlier-stage revision without an established invalidating finding.","drawbacks":["Separate evidence review adds preparation and interpretation effort.","Optional participation and minimal collection limit individual and subgroup conclusions.","Fresh tasks introduce language and task-comparability uncertainties."],"evidence_refs":[37,42,44,51],"flourishing":["Agency and dignity: passing, withholding and declining recording remain legitimate, not adverse outcomes.","Access and belonging: examine opportunities to inspect, contribute, correct and dissent across participation routes.","Sustainable workload and learning/transfer: examine teacher effort separately from task reasoning, retention and transfer."],"human_capabilities":["Reviewers must distinguish observation, interpretation, rival explanation and missing evidence.","Facilitators must preserve language supports, optional contribution, correction and dissent.","An accountable human owner and teacher, student and caregiver reviewers must examine proposed use and any separate inquiry before real participation."],"message":"Sequence 51 preserves human gates, but Evaluate also needs explicit rules for withholding conclusions when checks, comparison evidence or participation coverage are absent. Sequences 37 and 44 supply a candidate and protocol, not findings. I propose a claim-by-claim evidence ledger that separates untested behavior, task reasoning, access, workload and transfer. This is a Measurement expert draft for the rehearsal. No checks, participant observations, approvals or measured results exist; field evidence remains pending.","objections":["Hypothetical teacher dissent: “Paper cards support this discussion without administering another evaluation process.” Preserve paper-only delivery.","Rival explanations: fluent wording may reflect copying or recorder assistance; sparse wording may reflect reading, language, typing or discussion pressure.","Unknowns: task comparability, classroom languages, device access, discussion norms, coverage and acceptable workload."],"prototype_html":"","revisit_stage":"","risks":["An empty results record could be interpreted as evidence of no problems.","Copied limitations or group wording could be attributed to individual understanding.","Route selection, assistance and repeated exposure could produce misleading comparisons."],"system_capabilities":["Keep optional discussion wording separate from external evaluation records; collect no participant telemetry.","Expose invented evidence, supplied hints and correction without automatic reasoning judgments.","Preserve the paper route and synthetic interaction checks without learner identities, grades or rankings."],"tradeoffs":["Less explanatory cueing improves interpretation but can increase task demands.","Minimal collection protects dignity while limiting coverage claims.","Matching facilitation aids comparison but may obscure differences in ordinary workload."]},"seq":57,"stage":"evaluate"}]
