SEED: {"constraints":"No real student records, automated grades, ability ranking or inferred disability. Work on a shared device and support a teacher-facilitated paper alternative. Completion is not evidence of scientific reasoning. Investigate reading burden, device access and discussion norms. Teacher, student and caregiver review and any actual pilot remain pending. Require a complete small interactive HTML candidate, source-visible limits, and at least one meaningful revision after a challenge.","domain":"Middle-school science inquiry and reasoning from fictional evidence","idea":"Help middle-school science learners compare claims from conflicting observations, identify a changed condition, explain uncertainty and revise a claim. Begin by examining classroom conditions and needed reasoning, then build a small interactive fictional evidence workbench. Use a clearly labeled invented classroom data set about plant growth: different watering and lighting conditions make a simple taller-means-better claim uncertain. Treat all numbers as story inputs, never empirical study results. The candidate should let a learner inspect evidence, choose or write a claim, reveal a counterexample and revise their reasoning with transparent descriptive prompts.","people":"Students with varied reading and language access, science teachers, classroom support staff and caregivers; none are participants in this demonstration.","title":"Reasoning when evidence conflicts"}
CURRENT ARTIFACTS: {"build":{"content":{"body":"Status and basis
This is an executable candidate for a rehearsal, not an approved requirement or certified solution. Sequence 20 separates supported practice from transfer; 22 bounds visibility and delegation; 27 supplies a hypothetical replacement challenge; 29 revises Design; 35 specifies implementation transitions and handoff inspection. No real participant observations, authorization, measured results or demonstrated proficiency exist. Field evidence remains pending.

Build scope
The accompanying self-contained HTML displays A/B and reveals C on request. Every card labels its numbers as invented story inputs. The fictional period is 14 days; water uses arbitrary story units, not plant-care guidance. A: water 1, bright light, final height 12 cm, upright leaves. B: water 2, dim light, final height 18 cm, drooping leaves. C: water 2, bright light, final height 10 cm, upright leaves. Missing starting heights, plant type, soil and other conditions limit interpretation. Final height is not increase; leaf wording does not establish health; “better” needs a criterion.

Meaningful changes after challenges
Sequence 27 challenged unspecified insertion into occupied fields. Following 29, selecting a suggestion changes no writing; insertion names its target. Empty targets accept insertion directly. Occupied targets show current and proposed wording with Replace and Cancel. Replacement saves the immediately preceding wording for that target. Restoration shows both versions, requires confirmation and consumes its recovery copy. Independent initial/current recovery is supported. This is implemented behavior awaiting execution, not a verified benefit.
Sequence 35 challenges visible-field clearing as sufficient handoff assurance. Confirmed Clear recreates editable controls, resets selections and C, removes preview and dialog text, and discards pending and recovery objects. This addresses application-owned copies. It makes no secure-erasure claim about browser-managed undo, autofill, copies or what others saw. No earlier artifact needs another revision.

Interaction and output contract
Initial wording, current claim, comparison, support, uncertainty and dissent remain separately editable and optional. Retain, narrow, revise and withhold are descriptions, not required outcomes. Representation defaults to unresolved and never verifies agreement. Preview renders literal current entries and fixed limitations; blanks say “Text not supplied.” Editing or output-relevant selection changes remove the old preview. Evidence reveal alone preserves it. Native modal confirmation blocks other interactions; Cancel or Escape preserves entries, selections, evidence visibility, recovery and a valid preview. Focus returns to the initiating control. None of these behaviors has been tested here.

Human responsibilities and paper packet
A facilitator asks whose explanation is represented, exposes recorder wording for correction and preserves dissent. Roles are optional; passing preserves evidence access and later contribution. Learners may explain orally, draw, point with explanation or use supported recording without browser entry. Do not use browser wording as individual assessment evidence.
Paper-only delivery is a complete non-AI option. Copy the three labelled cards onto separate sheets and hold C until requested. Use optional response spaces: initial wording; current claim; compared pair and changed conditions; supporting observation; uncertainty; alternative wording/dissent; reconsideration; representation. Put suggested claims on separate slips. Ask “Which observation supports your wording?” Optional practice: “Choose another pair. What can you claim, and what remains uncertain?” Earlier prompts stay available. Continue on paper after device failure without mandatory re-entry. Paper visibility and route equivalence remain unresolved.

Review plan and next action
Proceed to synthetic interaction review in the rehearsal. Outside the candidate, use fictional wording to record expected behavior, observed behavior and unresolved defects. Check occupied insertion and Cancel; independent target recovery; replacement followed by typing and restoration/Cancel; preview invalidation; hide/reopen C; Escape and keyboard focus; clear cancellation and confirmed handoff; attempted recovery and browser undo afterward; literal rendering of markup-like text. Check small-screen reading and paper continuation. No checks have been run.
Before any real use, teacher, student and caregiver review and human interaction testing remain required. Investigate reading burden, language access, device availability, discussion norms and teacher effort separately from learning. Apparent lost uncertainty may reflect intentional replacement, recorder influence or spoken wording never entered. Plant differences could involve water, light, starting height or missing conditions. Completion and polished summaries do not establish reasoning, retention or transfer; separate inquiry requires human review. No pilot, launch or efficacy decision is made.","prototype_html":"<!doctype html><html lang="en"><head><meta charset="utf-8"><meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width,initial-scale=1"><title>Fictional evidence workbench</title><style>body{font:17px/1.5 system-ui,sans-serif;max-width:960px;margin:auto;padding:20px;color:#172638;background:#f7f8fa}h1,h2,h3{line-height:1.2}section,article,details{background:white;border:1px solid #aab5c0;border-radius:8px;padding:14px;margin:14px 0}.cards{display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:12px}.cards article{flex:1;min-width:190px}label{display:block;margin-top:12px;font-weight:600}textarea,select,button{font:inherit}textarea{display:block;box-sizing:border-box;width:100%;min-height:76px}select{max-width:100%}button{padding:8px 12px;margin:8px 6px 8px 0;cursor:pointer}button:disabled{cursor:default}button:focus-visible,textarea:focus-visible,select:focus-visible,summary:focus-visible{outline:3px solid #185ca0;outline-offset:3px}.notice{background:#edf3f8;padding:12px}.small{font-size:.9em}pre{white-space:pre-wrap;overflow-wrap:anywhere;font:inherit;margin:6px 0}dialog{max-width:650px;width:calc(100% - 70px);border:2px solid #34495e;border-radius:8px}dialog::backdrop{background:#0008}[hidden]{display:none!important}</style></head><body><h1>Reasoning when evidence conflicts</h1><p class="notice">Rehearsal candidate requiring human review and interaction testing. No learning results or approvals exist. No grades, rankings or automatic judgments.</p><section><h2>1. Inspect fictional evidence</h2><p>Fictional period: 14 days. Water amounts are arbitrary story units per day, not plant-care advice.</p><div class="cards"><article><h3>A</h3><p class="small">Invented classroom story: these numbers are story inputs, not study results.</p><p>Water: 1<br>Light: bright<br>Final height: 12 cm<br>Leaves: upright</p></article><article><h3>B</h3><p class="small">Invented classroom story: these numbers are story inputs, not study results.</p><p>Water: 2<br>Light: dim<br>Final height: 18 cm<br>Leaves: drooping</p></article><article id="cardC" hidden><h3>C — additional evidence</h3><p class="small">Invented classroom story: these numbers are story inputs, not study results.</p><p>Water: 2<br>Light: bright<br>Final height: 10 cm<br>Leaves: upright</p></article></div><button id="reveal" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="cardC">Reveal C: compare with another observation</button><p id="missing">Starting heights, plant type, soil and other conditions are unrecorded. Final height is not an increase. Leaf descriptions do not establish health. “Better” needs a criterion. These cards do not isolate causes.</p><details><summary>Optional comparison hint</summary><p>A/B differ in recorded water and light. With C visible, compare A/C and B/C. Similar recorded conditions do not establish equal unrecorded conditions. C challenges “more water always accompanies greater final height”; it does not refute every claim.</p></details></section><section><h2>2. Explain or discuss</h2><p class="notice">People nearby can see this screen. Use fictional evidence; do not enter names or personal details. You may discuss, draw or use paper without entering text. Passing preserves access and a later contribution opportunity.</p><p>What differs in observations and recorded conditions? Which observation supports your wording? All entries are optional. Initial wording is editable working text, not an authenticated first thought.</p><div id="work"><label for="suggestion">Optional suggested wording — inspecting it changes no entry</label><select id="suggestion"><option value="">Use my own wording in the fields below</option><option>B is taller at the end.</option><option>Taller means better.</option><option>I cannot decide yet.</option></select><label for="target">Insert suggestion into</label><select id="target"><option value="initial">Initial wording</option><option value="current">Current claim</option></select><button id="insert">Insert selected wording</button><p class="small">Suggestions are prompts, not verified answers. Occupied fields require confirmation.</p><label for="initial">Initial wording</label><textarea id="initial" autocomplete="off"></textarea><label for="current">Current claim — retain or change it as you choose</label><textarea id="current" autocomplete="off"></textarea><label for="comparison">Pair compared and changed recorded conditions</label><textarea id="comparison" autocomplete="off"></textarea><label for="support">Supporting observation</label><textarea id="support" autocomplete="off"></textarea><label for="uncertainty">Uncertainty or information needed</label><textarea id="uncertainty" autocomplete="off"></textarea><label for="dissent">Optional alternative wording or dissent</label><textarea id="dissent" autocomplete="off"></textarea><label for="reconsider">Reconsideration description</label><select id="reconsider"><option>Not selected</option><option>Retain</option><option>Narrow</option><option>Revise</option><option>Withhold</option></select><label for="representation">Whose explanation does this represent? No names.</label><select id="representation"><option>Representation unresolved</option><option>One explanation</option><option>Several explanations</option></select><p>These descriptions do not verify agreement. Invite correction and preserve disagreement.</p><h3>Temporary recovery within this group</h3><p>Only the preceding replaced wording for each target is retained. Restoration requires confirmation and consumes its copy.</p><button id="restoreInitial" disabled>Restore previous initial wording</button><pre id="savedInitial"></pre><button id="restoreCurrent" disabled>Restore previous current claim</button><pre id="savedCurrent"></pre></div><p>Optional practice: Choose another pair. What can you claim, and what remains uncertain? Earlier prompts remain available. This is supported practice, not evidence of independence or transfer.</p><button id="preview">Preview current discussion wording</button></section><section><h2>3. Correctable discussion preview</h2><p>Working wording for discussion; it does not verify contribution, agreement or learning. Correct the fields above or leave disagreement unresolved. Completion is not evidence of scientific reasoning.</p><div id="output" hidden></div></section><section><h2>Paper-only alternative</h2><p>A teacher can copy A/B/C onto separate sheets; reveal C here to prepare its sheet, then hold that sheet until requested in the paper activity. Every sheet keeps the invented-story label and missing-information notice. Put suggested claims on separate slips.</p><p>Optional response sheet: Initial wording; Current claim; Pair and changed conditions; Supporting observation; Uncertainty; Alternative wording/dissent; Retain/narrow/revise/withhold; Whose explanation is represented?</p><p>Discuss orally, draw, point with explanation or use supported recording. Make recorder wording available for correction. Continue on paper after device failure without mandatory re-entry. Paper also has visibility limits; equivalence between routes is unknown.</p></section><section><h2>Shared-device handoff</h2><p>Refresh or closing loses temporary writing. Clear removes application-owned entries, previews, pending proposals and recovery copies. It cannot retract copies or what others saw. Browser-managed undo and autofill remain untested; this is not secure erasure.</p><button id="clear">Clear for next group…</button></section><p id="status" role="status" aria-live="polite"></p><dialog id="confirm" aria-labelledby="dialogTitle"><h2 id="dialogTitle"></h2><pre id="dialogText"></pre><button id="accept">Confirm</button><button id="cancel">Cancel</button></dialog><script>/* Candidate only. No storage, telemetry, network or evaluation. Human interaction checks remain pending. */
const $=id=>document.getElementById(id),work=$('work'),template=work.innerHTML,dlg=$('confirm');
const fields=['initial','current','comparison','support','uncertainty','dissent'];
const labels={initial:'Initial wording',current:'Current claim',comparison:'Pair and changed conditions',support:'Supporting observation',uncertainty:'Uncertainty',dissent:'Alternative wording / dissent'};
let recovery={},pending=null,origin=null;
function say(s){$('status').textContent=s}
function invalidate(){const out=$('output');out.replaceChildren();out.hidden=true;say('Wording changed. Request a new preview when ready.')}
function recoverView(){for(const [id,suffix] of [['initial','Initial'],['current','Current']]){$('restore'+suffix).disabled=!(id in recovery);$('saved'+suffix).textContent=id in recovery?'Saved '+labels[id]+': '+recovery[id]:''}}
function ask(action,title,text,button,source){pending=action;origin=source;$('dialogTitle').textContent=title;$('dialogText').textContent=text;$('accept').textContent=button;dlg.showModal();$('cancel').focus()}
function finish(){pending=null;$('dialogTitle').textContent='';$('dialogText').textContent='';dlg.close();const focus=origin;origin=null;if(focus&&focus.isConnected)focus.focus()}
$('cancel').onclick=()=>finish();dlg.addEventListener('cancel',e=>{e.preventDefault();finish()});
work.addEventListener('input',e=>{if(e.target.tagName==='TEXTAREA')invalidate()});
work.addEventListener('change',e=>{if(['reconsider','representation'].includes(e.target.id))invalidate()});
work.addEventListener('click',e=>{if(pending)return;const id=e.target.id;if(id==='insert'){const text=$('suggestion').value,target=$('target').value;if(!text){say('Write your own wording directly, or select a suggestion.');return}if($(target).value===''){$(target).value=text;invalidate();return}ask({type:'replace',target,text},'Replace '+labels[target]+'?','Current wording:\n'+$(target).value+'\n\nProposed wording:\n'+text,'Replace',e.target)}else if(id==='restoreInitial'||id==='restoreCurrent'){const target=id==='restoreInitial'?'initial':'current';if(!(target in recovery))return;ask({type:'restore',target,text:recovery[target]},'Restore '+labels[target]+'?','Current wording:\n'+($(target).value||'Text not supplied')+'\n\nSaved wording:\n'+recovery[target]+'\n\nRestoration consumes this recovery copy.','Restore',e.target)}});
$('accept').onclick=()=>{if(!pending)return;if(pending.type==='clear'){work.innerHTML=template;recovery={};$('output').replaceChildren();$('output').hidden=true;$('cardC').hidden=true;$('reveal').textContent='Reveal C: compare with another observation';$('reveal').setAttribute('aria-expanded','false');finish();say('Application-owned writing, selections, preview, proposals and recovery cleared. No application undo crosses handoff.');return}const target=pending.target;if(pending.type==='replace')recovery[target]=$(target).value;else delete recovery[target];$(target).value=pending.text;invalidate();recoverView();finish()};
$('reveal').onclick=()=>{const card=$('cardC');card.hidden=!card.hidden;$('reveal').setAttribute('aria-expanded',String(!card.hidden));$('reveal').textContent=card.hidden?'Reveal C: compare with another observation':'Hide C; keep all writing';say(card.hidden?'C hidden; writing preserved.':'C revealed as additional evidence, not a verdict.')};
$('preview').onclick=()=>{const out=$('output');out.replaceChildren();for(const id of [...fields,'reconsider','representation']){const heading=document.createElement('h3'),text=document.createElement('pre');heading.textContent=labels[id]|| (id==='reconsider'?'Reconsideration':'Representation');text.textContent=$(id).value.trim()?$(id).value:'Text not supplied';out.append(heading,text)}const limits=document.createElement('p');limits.textContent=$('missing').textContent;out.append(limits);out.hidden=false;say('Literal preview ready. Review and correct it; no agreement or learning is verified.')};
$('clear').onclick=e=>ask({type:'clear'},'Clear for next group?','Remove all entries, selections, preview, pending proposals and recovery copies; hide C. No application restoration crosses handoff. Copies and what others saw cannot be removed. Cancel preserves the current activity.','Clear',e.target);
</script></body></html>","title":"Build candidate: fictional evidence, correction and shared-device handoff"},"content_hash":"0610d0b96ba67450d111048bf81b5bbc118f06e8c6c74bbe43f0b90b129fed9a","id":"build","parent_revision":null,"revision":1,"trigger_seq":37},"deploy":{"content":{"body":"Status and decision
This is an integrated Deploy artifact for a rehearsal, not an approved classroom requirement or release authorization. Sequence 37 supplies Build revision 1, which remains unexecuted. Sequence 44 supplies separate evidence streams and an external synthetic worksheet. Sequence 49 adds accountable decision ownership, reuse boundaries and pause procedures. No participant observations, human approvals, measured results or demonstrated proficiency exist. Field evidence remains pending.

Adopted deployment requirement
A review plan or successful technical check cannot authorize classroom use. Require a human decision record tied to the exact candidate revision, intended setting and narrowly stated scope. Review informs that decision; it does not substitute for it. This specifies Deploy without reopening an earlier artifact. The replacement and handoff changes documented in 37 remain candidate behaviors requiring testing.

Working decision record
Candidate: Build revision 1, sequence 37.
Intended setting, device/browser and scope: pending.
Accountable human decision owner: unassigned.
Teacher, student and caregiver review, including unresolved dissent: pending.
Synthetic findings, evidence locations and unresolved defects: pending; all checks untested.
Human interaction testing: pending.
Reading/language supports, device access and discussion arrangements: unresolved.
Paper preparation and facilitation capacity: pending review.
Participant collection or reuse: none authorized in this rehearsal.
Decision, rationale, limits and conditions for reconsideration: pending.
Record unresolved objections separately from the eventual decision; silence does not establish agreement. None of these fields constitutes an appointment or approval.

Human gates before real-use consideration
Technical review: use only fictional input and the worksheet in 44. Inspect suggestion insertion, occupied replacement, Cancel/Escape, independent recovery, restoration after editing, preview invalidation, literal rendering, evidence reveal, keyboard focus and handoff. Inspect browser undo/autofill separately. Record observed behavior separately from interpretation; no checks have run. Resolve discrepancies relevant to correction, access or exposure before proposing use.
Human review: teachers, students and caregivers must have opportunities to inspect wording, visibility, control comprehension and participation choices. Human interaction testing must examine the candidate and paper continuation. Review reading burden, language access, device availability, discussion norms and preparation/facilitation effort. Review participation separately from scientific reasoning; technical success establishes only tested behavior.
Decision gate: a designated human owner must document the permitted revision, setting and scope, remaining limitations and reasons for the decision. Any participant inquiry requires a separately reviewed purpose, minimum information, visibility, access, retention, correction and reuse process. None is supplied or approved here.

Facilitation and pause draft
Explain that the numbers are invented story inputs and the preview is correctable discussion wording. Ask whose explanation is represented; expose recorder wording for correction and preserve unresolved dissent. Allow retain, narrow, revise or withhold. Passing preserves evidence access and a later contribution opportunity. No learner must enter browser text or explain a route choice publicly.
If stale wording, inaccessible controls, unintended disclosure or pressure prevents participation, pause browser use and offer oral or paper continuation without mandatory re-entry. Invite removal of personal details without reading them aloud. Do not create an incident account in the candidate. Any real disclosure response requires an applicable human process, presently unspecified. Clearing cannot retract copies or what others saw and is not secure erasure.

Complete non-AI option
Prepare separate labelled A/B/C sheets from 37, keeping invented-story labels and missing-condition notices; hold C until requested. Use optional spaces for claim, comparison, support, uncertainty, dissent and reconsideration. Put suggestions on separate slips. Permit speech, drawing, pointing with explanation and supported recording. Make recorded wording correctable. Paper-only discussion is a legitimate choice; visibility, route equivalence and workload remain unresolved.

Learning interpretation and next action
Do not infer improvement from editable initial/current wording. Supplied limitations, copied prompts, completion and group summaries do not establish individual reasoning. Proposed reasoning lenses remain descriptive task evidence, never totals or proficiency judgments. Retention and transfer require separately reviewed fresh tasks; immediate supported performance establishes neither.
Sparse wording may reflect reading, typing, language demands, time or pressure; fluent wording may reflect copying, familiarity, assistance or recorder influence. Route selection and repeated exposure can confound comparisons. Plant differences may reflect water, light, starting height or missing conditions.
Next, prepare the blank decision packet and conduct the synthetic checks specified in 44 outside the candidate. Preserve paper-only dissent and unresolved gates. No launch, actual pilot, participant collection or efficacy decision follows from this artifact.","prototype_html":"","title":"Deploy synthesis: rehearsal decision packet and pending human gates"},"content_hash":"c379d174e2a620ce04940d278ba47027a5e634913276fcf27dd00b86f1ae8e92","id":"deploy","parent_revision":null,"revision":1,"trigger_seq":51},"design":{"content":{"body":"Status and basis
This is a rehearsal Design revision, not an approved classroom requirement. Sequence 24 integrates reasoning, participation, supported practice and visibility requirements; sequence 22 bounds delegation and disclosure. Sequence 27 is a hypothetical replacement event, not a technical finding or participant observation. No human approval, measured outcomes or demonstrated proficiency exist. All field evidence remains pending.

Revision responding to 27
Before: sequence 24 permits requested insertion of suggested claims but leaves occupied-field behavior unspecified. After: suggestions remain inspectable without changing writing. Insert directly only into an empty target. For occupied targets, identify the field, display current and proposed wording, and offer Replace and Cancel. Cancel preserves entries, selections, evidence visibility, recovery state and any valid preview. Design is the earliest affected artifact; this revision resolves that request without revisiting Understand or Model.

Evidence and layout
Use one responsive page: evidence, editable reasoning, then preview. Keep evidence accessible during editing. Use labelled native controls, visible focus, keyboard operation and information independent of color; accessibility remains unverified.
Each card states: “Invented classroom story: these numbers are story inputs, not study results.” Fictional period: 14 days. Water uses arbitrary story units per day, not plant-care advice.
A: water 1; bright light; final height 12 cm; upright leaves.
B: water 2; dim light; final height 18 cm; drooping leaves.
C: water 2; bright light; final height 10 cm; upright leaves.
Always disclose missing starting heights, plant type, soil and other conditions. Final height is not an increase; leaf descriptions do not establish health. “Better” requires a criterion.

Interaction and replacement contract
Begin with A/B. Offer “B is taller at the end,” “Taller means better,” “I cannot decide yet,” and original wording. An insertion action explicitly targets initial wording or current claim. Suggestions never imply correctness. Ask what differs in observations and recorded conditions; A/B differ in water and light.
Reveal C on request as additional evidence. Hiding or reopening C preserves writing. Invite A/C and B/C comparison without implying that recorded similarities isolate causation.
Maintain separately editable initial wording, current claim, comparison, support, uncertainty and optional dissent. Retain, narrow, revise and withhold are optional; neither text nor changed wording is required. Initial wording is working text, not an authenticated first thought.
Confirmed replacement retains only the immediately preceding wording for that target in temporary recovery memory. Offer Restore previous wording, showing its target and saved text. If restoring would displace subsequent edits, show current and saved wording with Restore and Cancel. Restoration is deliberate, consumes that recovery copy and never occurs automatically. Further replacement updates only that target's recovery copy. Replacement, restoration or editing invalidates the preview. Pending confirmation must resolve before another text-changing action.

Output, visibility and handoff
A requested preview assembles literal current entries, reconsideration choice and fixed missing-information text. Blanks say “Text not supplied.” Representation defaults to unresolved; one or several explanations are user descriptions, never verified agreement.
Beside entries: “People nearby can see this screen. Use fictional evidence; do not enter names or personal details. You may discuss, draw or use paper without entering text.” Beside preview: “Working wording for discussion; it does not verify contribution, agreement or learning. Correct it or leave disagreement unresolved.”
Clear for next group explains removal and offers Cancel and Clear. Cancel preserves state. Confirmed Clear removes entries, selections, preview, pending replacements and all recovery copies, resets C and announces clearing. No restoration crosses handoff or survives refresh. Clearing cannot retract copies or what others saw. Use no identities, participant notes, storage, telemetry, external resources or network calls. No grades, rankings, inferred disability or competence judgments.

Paper route, practice and gates
Copy A/B/C onto separate sheets; hold C until requested. Provide optional fields matching the browser entries and representation prompt. Put suggested claims on a separate sheet so inspection preserves original wording. Allow oral explanation, drawing and supported recording; expose recorder wording for correction. Passing preserves evidence access and later contribution. Continue on paper after device failure without mandatory re-entry. Paper also has visibility limits; route equivalence is unknown.
Ask “Which observation supports your wording?” Optional practice: “Choose another pair. What can you claim, and what remains uncertain?” Keep earlier prompts available. This is supported practice, not proof of independence. Separate delayed or cross-context inquiry requires human review and stays outside the candidate.
Apparent lost uncertainty could reflect intentional replacement, recorder influence or spoken wording never entered. Sparse responses may reflect reading, typing, time or pressure; fluent responses may reflect copying. Plant differences may involve water, light, starting height or missing conditions.
Build next. Before real use, require teacher, student and caregiver review and human interaction testing of insertion, Cancel, replacement, restoration after edits, preview invalidation, reveal, keyboard access, complete clearing and paper continuation. Examine workload and participation separately from learning.","prototype_html":"","title":"Revised Design: fictional evidence, deliberate replacement and recovery"},"content_hash":"b987da4a0b0a6891fefb42a9f75d46183ca421bd341d7c2c35452ab08056e5b7","id":"design","parent_revision":1,"revision":2,"trigger_seq":29},"evaluate":{"content":{"body":"Status and event
This is an integrated Evaluate artifact for the lifecycle rehearsal, not an approved inquiry protocol or classroom authorization. Sequence 62 hypothesizes a facilitator translating the missing-starting-height notice while adding the inference that greater final height does not establish greater growth. A reviewer then attributes that relation to the learner under a language-support label. No such interaction occurred. Build 37 remains unexecuted; technical checks, participant observations, measured results, human approvals and field evidence remain pending.

Decision and earliest revision
Request one targeted revision of Instrument 44. Its prospective records distinguish explanatory hints and language/access support but leave assistance serving both functions unspecified. Add mixed and unknown support content and a claim-specific attribution rule. Instrument is the earliest affected artifact because the gap concerns evidence recording and interpretation. The supplied event does not invalidate the fictional evidence or interaction specification. Carry the rule into Evaluate 59 now; the requested Instrument revision remains outstanding.

Replacement requirement for Instrument 44
For each reasoning claim, identify the particular relation being examined, such as final height versus growth increase. Describe assistance relative to that relation using access support, explanatory support, mixed support or support content unknown. Record what was supplied when known; a label such as translation cannot establish its reasoning contribution. Access support describes assistance making the evidence or wording available without supplying the target relation; explanatory support supplies that relation; mixed support does both. These are descriptions of assistance, never learner classifications or verified translations.
When the target relation was supplied, conclude only that the explanation occurred with that relation supplied. Independent production remains unestablished. When assistance content is unknown, withhold source attribution. Neither case establishes inability or lack of understanding. Do not infer absent assistance from an empty record. Keep support available; optional further explanation remains supported task evidence and cannot automatically establish independence or transfer.

Usable external record
Claim/relation: __. Candidate revision/task/context: __. Evidence stream: __. Supplied notices or hints: __. Assistance content and basis for description: pending. Support description relative to target: unknown. Learner contribution distinguishable from supplied content: unresolved. Observation and evidence location: pending. Rival explanation: __. Missing coverage: __. Permissible conclusion: unestablished. Dissent/correction: __. Next action: __.
Use invented wording for rehearsal. Any later participant collection must first satisfy 51's separately reviewed purpose, minimum-information, visibility, access, retention, correction and reuse arrangements. Do not silently convert the synthetic worksheet into a learner log.

Fictional classification exercises
These are invented paraphrase examples, not translations, observations or reviewer-agreement results. For the target relation final height versus growth increase: repeating “Starting heights are unrecorded” does not itself state that relation, although it supplies relevant information. Adding “Therefore B's final height does not show that it grew more” supplies the relation; if also providing language access, the expected draft description is mixed support. An account saying only “translation provided” leaves content unknown and cannot support source attribution. Classification must change with the target claim: the first example already supplies the missing-starting-height fact. Rehearse these distinctions outside the candidate; outcomes remain pending.

Evaluation boundaries and non-AI option
Preserve 57 and 59's separate technical, reasoning, participation and workload conclusions. Completion, copied limitations, editable initial/current wording and group previews establish neither individual improvement nor agreement. Existing understanding, imitation and newly adopted explanation remain rival explanations. Plant differences may involve water, light, starting height or missing conditions.
Teacher-facilitated paper discussion remains complete: labelled invented A/B/C cards, C held until requested, optional claim/comparison/uncertainty spaces and separate suggestion slips. Permit speech, drawing, passing and correctable supported recording. Apply identical attribution limits. Paper visibility, route equivalence and workload remain unknown.

Gates and next action
Revise only Instrument 44 with the requirement above, then rehearse the fictional cases alongside its synthetic worksheet and Evaluate's ledger. No checks were performed here. Teacher, student and caregiver review, human interaction testing, access and workload review, and designation of a decision owner remain pending. Any real use requires a revision-specific human decision; participant inquiry requires separate review. Withhold performance, efficacy, equity, retention and transfer conclusions.","prototype_html":"","title":"Evaluate response to 62: mixed assistance and bounded attribution"},"content_hash":"06b49eb265685399659435bc780b1514134489d23f388be7731f301e3905bc2a","id":"evaluate","parent_revision":1,"revision":2,"trigger_seq":64},"instrument":{"content":{"body":"Status and revision
This integrated Instrument rehearsal draft revises sequence 44 in response to hypothetical event 62 and the targeted request in 64. In that event, translation also supplies the final-height-versus-growth inference, yet a reviewer records only language support. Nothing occurred in a real classroom. Build 37 remains unexecuted. Technical checks, participant observations, measured results, human approvals and field evidence remain pending.

Changed requirement
Before: separate explanatory-hint and language/access fields leave mixed assistance unspecified. After: identify the exact claim or relation being examined and describe what assistance supplied relative to it. Instrument is the earliest affected artifact; this revision addresses that request without reopening another stage. Preserve 42 and 44's separate technical, reasoning, participation and workload records and 59/64's claim-ledger boundaries.

Assistance and attribution contract
Use access support, explanatory support, mixed support or support content unknown. Access support makes evidence or wording available without supplying the target relation; explanatory support supplies that relation; mixed support does both. Unknown means content cannot be established. Describe assistance, never classify learners or infer disability. Categories are contextual draft descriptions, not verified translations.
Inventory relevant supplied content: fixed notices, suggested wording, optional hints, facilitator explanation and recorder wording. Build already states that final height is not an increase; this is supplied reasoning too. Exposure does not prove uptake or understanding. A translation label alone cannot establish what reasoning was supplied, and an empty record cannot establish absent assistance.
When the target relation was supplied, conclude only that the explanation occurred with that relation supplied; independent production remains unestablished. When content is unknown, withhold source attribution. Neither establishes inability. Preserve supports. Optional further explanation remains supported task evidence, not automatic proof of independence or transfer.

External claim record
Claim/relation: __. Revision/task/context and route: __. Evidence stream: __. Fixed notices/hints available: __. Assistance content and basis: pending. Description relative to target: unknown. Contribution distinguishable from supplied content: unresolved. Observation: pending. Evidence location: pending. Interpretation: __. Rival explanation: __. Missing coverage: __. Permissible conclusion: unestablished. Dissent/correction: __. Next action: __.
Use invented wording in rehearsal. Any participant collection requires 51's separately reviewed purpose, minimum information, visibility, access, retention, correction and reuse arrangements. Do not convert synthetic checks into learner logs.

Fictional attribution exercises
Target: final height versus growth increase. Repeating “Starting heights are unrecorded” supplies relevant information but does not itself state that relation; access-only description requires evidence about the actual assistance content. Adding “B being taller does not show that it grew more” supplies the relation; if also enabling language access, the proposed description is mixed. “Translation provided” alone leaves content unknown. If the target is identifying missing starting heights, the first sentence already supplies it. These are invented paraphrases and draft interpretations, not verified translations or reviewer-agreement results. Rehearsal outcomes remain pending.

Synthetic technical worksheet and checks
For each check: candidate 37; browser/version __; viewport/input __; starting state __; fictional input __; action __; expected behavior __; observed behavior pending; evidence location pending; outcome untested; discrepancy __; question __. Later technical outcomes describe only tested behavior and conditions.
Check suggestion inspection and empty insertion; occupied replacement and Cancel/Escape; independent recovery; restoration after typing and cancellation; preview invalidation; literal markup-like text; reveal/hide preservation; canceled and confirmed clearing; removal of application recovery; keyboard focus, narrow-screen reading and paper continuation. Inspect browser undo/autofill separately without claiming secure erasure. No checks have run.

Reasoning, access and workload
Describe claim-observation connections, condition comparisons and uncertainty as task evidence, ambiguous, not elicited or not observed, with assistance boundaries. No totals, grades, rankings or proficiency labels. Editable initial/current wording, completion and group previews establish neither improvement nor individual understanding. Examine inspection, contribution, correction and dissent opportunities alongside preparation, facilitation and recovery effort. Missing complaints establish no equity conclusion. Routes, languages and discussion conditions remain unknown.

Paper route and next action
The complete non-AI option uses labelled invented A/B/C cards, C held until requested, optional matching response spaces and separate suggestion slips. Permit speech, drawing, passing and correctable supported recording. Apply identical attribution limits; route equivalence remains unknown. Any comparison needs reviewed distinct tasks and documented support differences; delayed retention and fresh-task transfer require separate inquiry.
Next rehearse the attribution cases and technical worksheet outside the candidate, recording actual outcomes only when available. Teacher, student and caregiver review, human interaction testing and revision-specific human decisions remain pending. No real use, collection, efficacy or learning conclusion follows.","prototype_html":"","title":"Instrument revision: claim-specific assistance and bounded attribution"},"content_hash":"8b08312e0af95d8373938da9cb1c46b4f18aaddfd78ff960e0ce292adc551c7e","id":"instrument","parent_revision":1,"revision":2,"trigger_seq":67},"model":{"content":{"body":"Status and basis
This is the integrated Model artifact for a rehearsal, not an approved classroom requirement. Sequence 4 establishes reconsideration and causal uncertainty; sequence 6 establishes correction and participation beyond device control; sequence 8 supplies the fictional evidence and revised Understand specification; sequence 13 supplies transition, representation and recovery rules. No participant observations, human approval, measured outcomes or demonstrated proficiency exist.

Reasoning model
The intended practice is to connect a claim to observations, compare recorded conditions, identify missing information and reconsider an explanation. Observation, interpretation and causal explanation must remain distinguishable. Reconsideration may retain, narrow, revise or withhold a claim. Completion, response length and changed wording do not establish understanding or transfer.

Evidence model
Every card displays: “Invented classroom story: these numbers are story inputs, not study results.” The fictional period is 14 days. Water amounts are arbitrary story units per day, not plant-care advice.
A: water 1; bright light; final height 12 cm; upright leaves.
B: water 2; dim light; final height 18 cm; drooping leaves.
C: water 2; bright light; final height 10 cm; upright leaves.
Starting heights, plant type, soil and other conditions are unrecorded. These are final heights, not measured increases. Leaf descriptions do not validate health. “Better” requires a stated criterion and remains open to dispute.

Interaction and output model
1. Inspect A and B. Select or write an initial claim, including “I cannot decide yet.” Ask what differs in observations and recorded conditions; both water and light differ. Keep evidence available during writing.
2. Explicitly reveal C as additional evidence. C challenges a generalization that more water always accompanies greater final height; it does not refute every claim. Compare A/C and B/C. Recorded similarities do not establish that unrecorded conditions were equal. Returning to the earlier view preserves all writing; C remains available to reopen.
3. Reconsider using retain, narrow, revise or withhold. Maintain separately editable initial wording, current claim, supporting observations, uncertainty and optional alternative wording/dissent. No entry or changed claim is required to continue. Empty means text not supplied.
4. A user-triggered preview produces a discussion summary of current wording, supporting observations, entered uncertainty and dissent, alongside the story's missing information. Use neutral prompts, not automatic evaluation. Show “Text not supplied” for blank entries. Permit return to editing without loss. Initial wording remains editable and is not an authenticated record of a first thought.

Representation and transition rules
Offer descriptions such as “one explanation,” “several explanations” and “representation unresolved,” without names. State that these descriptions do not verify agreement. Ask whose explanation the wording represents and invite correction; never certify a group claim. Passing preserves evidence access and a later contribution opportunity.
Ordinary navigation, reveal and preview preserve entries. Clear for next group first explains that claims, uncertainty and dissent will be removed, then offers Cancel and Clear. Cancel preserves everything. Clear removes visible entries and temporary summary copies, resets reveal state and announces the result. No undo crosses the handoff boundary. Use no storage, network submission or identity fields. Explain that refresh or closing loses temporary writing. Shared-screen privacy remains limited.

Human responsibilities and non-AI route
Teachers facilitate competing explanations, make recorder wording available for correction and allow optional roles without required reading aloud or device operation. Learners may use oral explanation, drawing, writing or supported recording. Do not infer disability or ability from route choice.
Design must supply ready-to-use paper cards A/B/C and a sheet with the same claim, comparison, support, uncertainty, reconsideration and dissent prompts. Reveal paper C at the matching step. On device failure, continue on paper without mandatory re-entry. Paper-only delivery is a legitimate option; route equivalence is unverified.

Revision, uncertainty and gates
Adopt sequence 13's meaningful change: replace an unspecified clear action with cancelable clearing, explicit preservation rules and a handoff boundary; replace implied shared agreement with unverified representation descriptions.
Sparse writing may reflect reading, vocabulary, typing, time, discussion pressure or comparison difficulty; polished writing may reflect copying or recorder fluency. Story differences may reflect water, light, their combination, starting height or other missing conditions. Field evidence remains pending.
Advance to Design to specify layout and prepared paper materials. Before real use, require teacher, student and caregiver review, human review of the browser candidate and interaction testing of reveal, correction, cancellation, clearing, keyboard access and paper continuation. Investigate participation and teacher effort separately from learning; transfer requires a separate reasoning task. No launch or efficacy claim is authorized by this rehearsal.","prototype_html":"","title":"Model synthesis: evidence, reconsideration and shared-device recovery"},"content_hash":"32ff68db1c645f3e1587550034f5475d52e83993dffbd5228bc723d62617b5ae","id":"model","parent_revision":null,"revision":1,"trigger_seq":15},"understand":{"content":{"body":"Status and basis
This is a usable rehearsal specification, not an approved classroom requirement. The seed supplies the purpose and constraints. Sequence 4 contributes reasoning and causal-uncertainty requirements; sequence 6 contributes representation, access and workload requirements. No participant observations, human approval, empirical success or demonstrated proficiency exist.

Purpose and boundaries
Help learners compare observations, identify changed recorded conditions, explain uncertainty and reconsider a claim. Height is one observation; “better” remains undefined unless the learner specifies a criterion. Activity completion does not establish scientific reasoning, ability or transfer. Exclude real student records, automated grades, ability ranking and inferred disability.

Draft fictional evidence cards
Every card must say: “Invented classroom story: these numbers are story inputs, not study results.” All heights are end-of-story heights in centimetres after a fictional 14-day period. Water amounts are arbitrary story units per day; they are not plant-care instructions.
A: water 1 unit; light labelled bright; height 12 cm; leaves described as upright.
B: water 2 units; light labelled dim; height 18 cm; leaves described as drooping.
C: water 2 units; light labelled bright; height 10 cm; leaves described as upright.
Starting heights, plant type, soil and other conditions were not recorded. Leaf descriptions are observations within the story, not validated measures of health. No card establishes which plant is “better” or why heights differ.

Activity sequence
1. Inspect A and B. Ask: “What differs in the observations? Which recorded conditions changed?” Both watering and lighting differ. Offer claims such as “B is taller at the end,” “Taller means better,” original wording, or “I cannot decide yet.” Ask what “better” means if that term is used.
2. Reveal C as an additional evidence card and keep A and B visible. C challenges a possible generalization that more water always accompanies greater final height; it does not prove a causal effect. Compare A with C and B with C, identifying recorded differences and missing information.
3. Ask: “Which observation supports your claim? What remains unresolved? Would you retain, narrow, revise or withhold it?” Keep the initial wording alongside the reconsidered explanation, both editable. A learner may retain a qualified claim with reasons. Do not issue correctness, completion or proficiency judgments.

Meaningful revisions adopted from the challenges
Sequence 4: replace the seed’s expected claim revision with reconsideration, including retention and withholding. Compare both watering and lighting and remove any implication that height determines overall quality.
Sequence 6: replace presumed equitable rotating roles with optional, adaptable roles and contribution routes that require neither reading aloud nor device operation. Before calling an explanation shared, ask whose explanation it represents, invite wording corrections and preserve unresolved dissent. Passing does not remove access to evidence or a later contribution opportunity.
These are documented specification changes in the rehearsal; their benefits remain unverified.

Participation and non-AI route
Use the identical cards and prompts in a teacher-facilitated paper discussion. Allow oral explanation, drawing, writing and supported recording. A recorder makes the wording available for correction. Do not demand public explanations for passing or choosing another route. Provide a deliberate clear action between device groups. Paper-only delivery remains a legitimate design choice; equivalence across routes is pending review.

Rival explanations and unknowns
A sparse response may reflect reading burden, typing effort, vocabulary, time, discussion pressure or difficulty comparing evidence. A detailed response may reflect copying or recorder fluency. Neither establishes understanding. Plant-height differences could involve water, light, their combination, starting height or other unrecorded differences. Classroom languages, device availability, support staffing, discussion norms and preparation time remain unknown.

Evidence and gates
Next, Model should map observations, claims, uncertainty, reconsideration and representation checks into an interaction specification and matching paper activity. Carry dissent and unknowns forward. Before real use, require teacher, student and caregiver review plus human review of the browser candidate and interaction testing. Examine who can inspect, contribute, correct and dissent, and the effort required from teachers. Record observations separately from interpretations; investigate transfer with a separate reasoning task. All field evidence and any actual pilot remain pending.","prototype_html":"","title":"Understand synthesis: reasoning when fictional evidence conflicts"},"content_hash":"25360875f427f69409e0069ca586e1172ed6abf9dd08c39d95c0f718a0f85cf3","id":"understand","parent_revision":null,"revision":1,"trigger_seq":8}}
RECENT PUBLIC DISCUSSION: [{"agent":"measurement","response":{"advantages":["Separate evidence streams prevent interaction success becoming a learning claim.","Descriptive task lenses accept justified retention, qualification and withholding.","Coverage checks expose access and workload unknowns without ranking learners."],"artifact_body":"Status and basis
This is an expert draft for the rehearsal, not an approved requirement or inquiry protocol. Sequence 20 separates supported practice from transfer; 22 excludes participant notes from the candidate; 35 proposes synthetic review; 37 supplies an unexecuted Build candidate. No participant observations, approvals, measured results or demonstrated proficiency exist. Field evidence remains pending.

Measurement challenge and requirement change
Before: sequence 37 names interaction checks and separate learning inquiry but leaves evidence interpretation and comparison procedures unspecified.
After: require separate technical, reasoning, participation and workload records. A technical pass supports only the tested behavior. A discussion preview is editable recorded wording, not an authenticated baseline, individual assessment or evidence of agreement. Do not calculate improvement from its initial/current fields. Keep fixed missing-information text separate from learner-supplied explanations: displaying or copying that notice does not demonstrate understanding. This adds an Instrument requirement without reopening Build.

Synthetic reviewer worksheet
Use fictional text only, outside the candidate. One row per check: candidate revision; browser/viewport/input method; starting state; action; expected behavior; observed behavior; evidence location; discrepancy; unresolved question. Observed behavior and evidence location remain blank until execution. Classify outcomes as observed as expected, discrepancy or untested; these are technical descriptions, not learner scores.
Probe occupied insertion followed by Cancel; independent recovery for each target; restoration after further editing; preview invalidation; reveal/hide preservation; Escape and focus return; clear cancellation; confirmed clearing followed by attempted recovery and browser undo; literal markup-like text. Record application behavior separately from browser-managed history. No check has been run and no secure-erasure assurance follows.

Draft reasoning lenses for later human review
Seek an explanation connecting a claim to a specific observation; identifying recorded similarities and differences; distinguishing final height from increase; and explaining how missing conditions limit causal conclusions. Reconsideration may retain, narrow, revise or withhold with reasons. Treat each lens as evidenced in this task, ambiguous, not elicited or not observed, with a brief supporting example if a separately approved inquiry permits collection. No totals, grades, rankings or proficiency labels.
Accept speech, drawing, pointing with explanation, writing and supported recording. An empty browser field means text not supplied. A group explanation cannot establish individual capability. Correction, dissent and requests for support remain valid actions rather than failures.

Comparison proposal, not an authorized study
Use teacher-facilitated paper discussion as the complete non-AI comparison option. Match fictional evidence, available time, facilitator prompts and access supports as far as practicable; document differences rather than asserting route equivalence. Compare reasoning opportunities, correction, participation and teacher effort separately.
Self-selected routes cannot isolate a device effect. If a later reviewed inquiry compares routes within learners or groups, propose counterbalanced order and distinct matched fictional tasks; repeated A/B/C exposure risks practice effects. Any assignment method requires review. Report assistance, exposure, missing observations and implementation differences alongside findings. Neither synthetic checks nor a before/after discussion licenses a causal efficacy claim.

Transfer and retention
Sequence 20's cart task repeats the plant story's numerical pattern, so success could reflect pattern matching. Draft a fresh fictional task with a different evidence structure: for example, a challenged causal claim with no direct counterexample, requiring withholding because conditions differ. Review its language and scientific interpretation before use. Begin with an open prompt; retain evidence visibility and access supports, and distinguish explanatory hints from language assistance. A later task at a reviewed delay addresses retention separately. Immediate supported performance establishes neither retention nor transfer.

Coverage, workload and gates
Prepare a synthetic coverage matrix crossing paper/shared-device routes, reading and language presentation, contribution modes and discussion arrangements. Mark each cell planned, checked or unknown; no cells are checked here. Later coverage must use only reviewed, voluntary information, never inferred disability or ability. No observed difference cannot establish equity when relevant coverage is absent.
Review preparation, facilitation, correction and recovery effort alongside opportunities to inspect, contribute and dissent. Preserve disagreement about whether instrumentation is worth its burden. Any real collection needs a separately reviewed purpose, minimum information, access, retention and correction process. Teacher, student and caregiver review and human interaction testing remain pending; no pilot or launch is authorized.","artifact_title":"Instrument expert draft: task evidence, comparison and coverage","challenged_assumption":"Successful interactions and polished discussion wording can support claims about reasoning, transfer or equitable access without a specified comparison and coverage method.","decision":"Continue Instrument by preparing the external synthetic worksheet and comparison/coverage drafts for review. Execute no checks or participant inquiry in this turn; retain all human gates and field evidence as pending.","drawbacks":["Manual review and separate transfer tasks require facilitator time.","Shared wording cannot establish each learner's reasoning.","Comparable tasks and participation routes remain unvalidated."],"evidence_refs":[20,22,35,37],"flourishing":["Agency and dignity: declining recording, retaining claims and dissent remain legitimate.","Access and belonging: investigate who can inspect, contribute and correct across routes.","Sustainable workload and learning/transfer: examine facilitation effort separately from reasoning and delayed performance."],"human_capabilities":["Reviewers must distinguish observed task behavior, interpretation and missing evidence.","Facilitators must preserve access supports, correction, dissent and optional participation.","Teacher, student and caregiver reviewers must examine any proposed inquiry before real use."],"message":"Sequence 37 proposes synthetic interaction review, but technical correctness cannot establish reasoning, equitable participation or transfer. Instrument should separate these evidence streams and specify a comparison method before any learning claim. This Measurement expert draft adds descriptive task lenses, comparison safeguards and coverage checks. No checks were executed; human review and all field evidence remain pending.","objections":["Hypothetical teacher dissent: paper-only discussion may leave more time for reasoning than instrument administration.","Rivals: apparent improvement may reflect cue familiarity, copying, recorder influence or teacher assistance.","Unknowns: language access, reading burden, device availability, discussion norms and task comparability."],"prototype_html":"","revisit_stage":"","risks":["Copied limitations or fluent recorder wording may resemble reasoning evidence.","Route selection, teacher assistance and prior familiarity can confound comparisons.","Absent subgroup evidence may be misreported as equitable access."],"system_capabilities":["Keep discussion previews separate from any external review worksheet; add no participant telemetry.","Expose evidence, optional hints and correction without scores or competence judgments.","Support synthetic transition checks and matching paper materials without personal records."],"tradeoffs":["Comparable prompts improve interpretation but can reproduce cueing.","Minimal collection protects dignity but limits subgroup conclusions.","Fresh transfer tasks reduce rehearsal effects but introduce unfamiliar vocabulary."]},"seq":42,"stage":"instrument"},{"agent":"orchestrator","response":{"advantages":["Separate evidence streams prevent technical checks from becoming learning claims.","Explicit expected behaviors make correction, cancellation and handoff reviewable.","Paper-only comparison preserves a complete non-AI option."],"artifact_body":"This is an integrated Instrument draft for a rehearsal, not an approved inquiry protocol. Sequence 37 supplies the unexecuted Build candidate; 35 specifies transition review; 42 adds separate evidence streams, comparison safeguards and coverage. Sequences 20 and 22 bound learning claims and collection. No real observations, measured results, approvals or demonstrated proficiency exist. Field evidence remains pending.

Adopted Instrument change: replace an unspecified interpretation of interaction checks with explicit evidence boundaries. Technical behavior, reasoning, participation and workload require separate records. Initial/current entries are editable working wording: do not calculate learning improvement from them. Fixed missing-information text must remain distinguishable from an explanation supplied by a learner; displaying or copying it does not demonstrate understanding. This changes Instrument without requiring revision of an earlier artifact.

Synthetic worksheet, outside the candidate: duplicate this blank record for each check. Candidate: Build revision 1, sequence 37. Browser/version: __. Viewport/input method: __. Starting state: __. Fictional input: __. Action: __. Expected behavior: __. Observed behavior: pending. Evidence location: pending. Outcome: untested. Discrepancy: __. Unresolved question: __. After execution, outcomes may be described as observed as expected, discrepancy or untested. Record observation separately from interpretation. Use only invented text; no participant accounts or browser telemetry.

Correction checks: enter “B is taller at the end, but starting heights are unknown.” Inspect a suggestion: writing should remain unchanged. Insert into an empty target: only that target should change. Insert into an occupied target: confirmation should show its current and proposed wording. Cancel or Escape should preserve entries, selections, reveal state, recovery and a valid preview, then return focus. Confirm replacement, edit again and request restoration: show current and saved wording; Cancel preserves both; confirmed restoration consumes that target's recovery copy. Test initial/current recovery independently. All outcomes remain untested.

Preview and handoff checks: preview invented markup-like text such as <b>story</b>; expect literal text rather than formatting. Editing or output-relevant selection changes should remove the preview; reveal/hide C alone should preserve it and all writing. Cancel Clear should preserve state. Confirm Clear should reset entries, selections, C, preview, dialog text, pending proposals and recovery; attempted application restoration should be unavailable. Separately inspect browser undo/autofill after handoff and record any retained wording without claiming secure erasure. Check keyboard focus, narrow-screen reading and paper continuation. No checks have run.

Prospective reasoning record, requiring separate human review before collection: task and context __; participation mode __; explanatory hints __; language/access support __; observation __; interpretation __; rival explanation __; missing evidence __. Proposed lenses are connecting a claim to a specific observation, identifying recorded similarities/differences, distinguishing final height from increase, and explaining limits from missing conditions. Describe each as evidenced in this task, ambiguous, not elicited or not observed, with permitted supporting evidence. No totals, grades, rankings or proficiency labels. Retention, narrowing, revision and withholding can each be reasoned responses. Blank fields and group wording cannot establish individual capability.

Comparison and transfer draft: paper-only teacher-facilitated discussion is the complete non-AI option. Match fictional evidence, time, prompts and supports where practicable; document differences. Self-selected routes cannot isolate a device effect. Any later comparison should review distinct matched tasks and counterbalanced order rather than repeat A/B/C unchanged. Sequence 20's cart pattern may invite pattern matching; review a fresh fictional task where differing conditions leave a causal claim unresolved without a direct counterexample. Keep evidence and language supports available. A separately reviewed delayed task addresses retention; immediate supported performance establishes neither retention nor transfer.

Coverage and workload record: route __; reading/language presentation __; contribution mode __; discussion arrangement __; evidence of inspection/correction/dissent opportunities __; preparation/facilitation/recovery effort __; unresolved barrier __. Every entry is presently planned or unknown. Do not infer disability, ability or route equivalence. Sparse wording may reflect reading, typing, time or pressure; fluent wording may reflect copying, familiarity, assistance or recorder influence. Preserve the hypothetical objection that paper alone may justify less administration.

Next action: conduct synthetic review and resolve documented discrepancies before considering deployment. Technical findings would justify revisiting the earliest artifact they actually invalidate; none is established now. Before real use or collection, teacher, student and caregiver review, human interaction testing and a reviewed purpose, minimum information, access, retention and correction process remain pending. No pilot, launch or efficacy claim follows from this rehearsal.","artifact_title":"Instrument synthesis: synthetic review and bounded evidence interpretation","challenged_assumption":"Successful interactions, editable discussion wording and absent access complaints provide sufficient evidence of learning, transfer or equitable participation.","decision":"Adopt this Instrument draft and proceed to synthetic interaction review outside the candidate. Keep deployment and participant inquiry pending until applicable human gates and unresolved defects are addressed.","drawbacks":["Manual review and separate reasoning inquiry require time.","Editable group wording cannot establish individual learning.","Task comparability and participation coverage remain unvalidated."],"evidence_refs":[20,22,35,37,42],"flourishing":["Agency and dignity: declining recording, withholding claims and correcting wording remain legitimate.","Access and belonging: examine opportunities to inspect, contribute and dissent across device, oral, drawing and paper routes.","Sustainable workload and learning/transfer: examine preparation and facilitation effort separately from reasoning, retention and transfer."],"human_capabilities":["Reviewers must distinguish expected behavior, observed behavior, interpretation and missing evidence.","Facilitators need prepared materials and time to preserve correction, dissent, access supports and optional participation.","Teacher, student and caregiver reviewers need opportunities to challenge inquiry purpose, visibility, burden and reuse before real use."],"message":"Instrument integrates Build sequence 37 and Measurement sequence 42, preserving the learning and visibility limits in sequences 20 and 22. The artifact below supplies an external synthetic review worksheet, expected behaviors, separate evidence streams and pending human gates. No checks, participant inquiry or approvals exist. Next, conduct synthetic interaction review; technical success would support only the behaviors tested.","objections":["Hypothetical teacher dissent: paper-only discussion may provide more reasoning time than instrument administration.","Hypothetical learner dissent: visible disagreement may identify its author despite absent name fields.","Unknowns: reading burden, languages, device availability, discussion norms and acceptable review effort."],"prototype_html":"","revisit_stage":"","risks":["Copied limitations or recorder fluency could be mistaken for reasoning.","Route selection, assistance and repeated exposure could confound comparisons.","Technical passes or missing observations could be misreported as accessibility or equity."],"system_capabilities":["Keep optional editable reasoning and literal previews separate from external review records; collect no participant telemetry.","Support synthetic checks of replacement, recovery, preview invalidation, cancellation and handoff.","Display fictional evidence and limitations without grades, rankings, inferred disability or competence judgments."],"tradeoffs":["Minimal collection protects dignity but limits conclusions about access.","Matched prompts aid comparison but may reproduce cueing.","Recovery protects qualifications while adding controls and copies to inspect."]},"seq":44,"stage":"instrument"},{"agent":"responsible","response":{"advantages":["Explicit decision ownership makes unresolved gates reviewable.","Correction and paper continuation preserve learner choice.","Restricted reuse reduces informal assessment and surveillance."],"artifact_body":"Status and basis
This is a Responsible AI expert draft for a rehearsal, not an approved deployment requirement. Sequence 22 bounds visibility and delegation; 35 challenges handoff assurances; 37 supplies an unexecuted candidate; 42 separates evidence streams; 44 specifies pending review. No real participant observations, human approvals, measured outcomes or demonstrated proficiency exist. All field evidence remains pending.

Challenge and proposed Deploy change
Before: sequence 44 requires review before deployment and collection but does not specify who owns a release decision, what use that decision covers, or how use stops when correction or privacy fails.
After: require an external human decision record tied to the exact candidate revision and intended setting. Review is an input to a decision, not authorization by itself. Synthetic technical success cannot establish learning, equitable access or classroom readiness. This adds a Deploy requirement; no earlier artifact requires revision on the supplied evidence.

Blank decision record
Candidate: Build revision 1, sequence 37. Intended setting and scope: pending. Accountable human decision owner: unassigned. Teacher, student and caregiver review: pending. Synthetic interaction findings and unresolved defects: pending. Human interaction testing: pending. Access, reading and language arrangements: unresolved. Prepared paper route and facilitation capacity: pending review. Permitted recording or reuse: none authorized in this rehearsal. Decision, rationale and limitations: pending. No entry implies an actual appointment, approval or launch.

Delegation boundary
The candidate may display invented plant evidence, reveal C on request, insert suggested wording deliberately and assemble a literal discussion preview. Humans remain responsible for facilitation and any consequential decisions. Browser wording must not determine grades, ability, inferred disability, agreement, access to instruction or progression. Blank fields mean text not supplied. Initial/current wording is editable and cannot authenticate improvement. Fixed limitation text is supplied scaffolding, not learner reasoning.

Collection and reuse boundary
No storage in the candidate does not control screenshots, copying, classroom observation or external notes. Removing names does not establish anonymity. Keep sequence 44's synthetic worksheet restricted to invented inputs and technical behavior. Do not silently convert it into a participant log. Any proposed real inquiry requires separate human review of purpose, minimum information, visibility, access, retention, correction and proposed reuse. Participation or choosing a route does not establish permission to collect or reuse an explanation.

Correction and pause draft
Before shared-device handoff, a facilitator offers correction without requiring agreement or naming the dissenter. If unintended personal details appear, invite removal without reading them aloud; do not create an incident record in the candidate. If stale wording, inaccessible controls, exposure or pressure prevents safe participation, pause browser use and offer paper or oral continuation without mandatory re-entry. A real disclosure response would require the school's human process, which is not supplied or approved here. Clearing cannot retract copies or what others saw; browser undo and autofill remain untested.

Non-AI option and dissent
Teacher-facilitated paper discussion using the labelled fictional A/B/C cards and optional comparison, uncertainty and reconsideration prompts is a complete non-AI option. Keep suggested claims on separate slips and recorder wording available for correction. Learners may speak, draw or pass without browser entry. Paper visibility, route equivalence and preparation burden remain unresolved. Preserve the hypothetical teacher objection that paper alone may better fit available discussion time.

Rivals, evidence and next action
Sparse wording may reflect reading, typing, language demands, time or pressure; fluent wording may reflect copying, assistance or recorder influence. Silence cannot establish agreement or absence of privacy concerns. Plant differences could reflect water, light, starting height or missing conditions. Next, prepare this decision record and conduct the synthetic checks specified in 44 before any real-use consideration. Human review and field inquiry remain pending. A later decision must state its narrow scope and unresolved evidence; no launch or efficacy claim follows from this draft.","artifact_title":"Deploy expert draft: accountable gates, bounded reuse and pause procedures","challenged_assumption":"A review plan, technical checks and progression to Deploy sufficiently establish authority and readiness for classroom use or reuse of discussion wording.","decision":"Continue Deploy only as a rehearsal: prepare the accountable decision record and complete pending synthetic review. Keep classroom use and participant collection pending human gates.","drawbacks":["Review and handoff supervision require facilitator time.","Shared screens expose wording despite absent names.","Minimal records limit retrospective investigation."],"evidence_refs":[22,35,37,42,44],"flourishing":["Agency and dignity: declining recording, withholding and correction require no public justification.","Access and belonging: preserve oral, drawing and paper participation, including unresolved disagreement.","Sustainable workload and learning/transfer: review facilitation effort separately from completion or reasoning claims."],"human_capabilities":["An accountable human decision owner must review unresolved gates and document the permitted scope before real use.","Facilitators need time to support correction, dissent, optional recording and paper continuation.","Teacher, student and caregiver reviewers need accessible opportunities to challenge visibility, reading burden and workload."],"message":"Deployment readiness is unestablished: sequence 44 specifies review, but no checks or human approvals exist. This Responsible AI expert draft adds accountable decision ownership, limits on reuse and a pause procedure. Progress through rehearsal stages must not become permission for classroom use. Field evidence remains pending.","objections":["Hypothetical learner dissent: “My disagreement identifies me even without my name.” Offer contribution without screen entry.","Hypothetical teacher dissent: paper-only discussion may justify less administration.","Unknowns include classroom visibility, reading and language access, device settings, discussion norms and acceptable workload."],"prototype_html":"","revisit_stage":"","risks":["Stage progression could be mistaken for classroom authorization.","Screenshots or external worksheets could create identifiable learner histories.","Representation labels and fluent previews could become assessment proxies."],"system_capabilities":["Limit delegation to fictional evidence display, requested insertion and literal previews; provide no consequential judgments.","Preserve correction and cancellation; remove application-owned copies at confirmed handoff without claiming secure erasure.","Collect no identities, participant telemetry or learner histories; keep synthetic review separate."],"tradeoffs":["Recovery protects qualifications but retains additional visible copies.","Review records support accountability but can become learner records.","Paper avoids device management while retaining visibility and workload concerns."]},"seq":49,"stage":"deploy"},{"agent":"orchestrator","response":{"advantages":["Revision-specific gates make missing evidence and decision ownership explicit.","Correction, optional recording and paper continuation preserve participation choices.","Separate technical and learning evidence limits unsupported conclusions."],"artifact_body":"Status and decision
This is an integrated Deploy artifact for a rehearsal, not an approved classroom requirement or release authorization. Sequence 37 supplies Build revision 1, which remains unexecuted. Sequence 44 supplies separate evidence streams and an external synthetic worksheet. Sequence 49 adds accountable decision ownership, reuse boundaries and pause procedures. No participant observations, human approvals, measured results or demonstrated proficiency exist. Field evidence remains pending.

