. Adopt a rehearsal deployment packet with explicit human gates, correction and pause procedures, and a complete paper option. Next, prepare synthetic review records and resolve discrepancies before any real-use decision. No earlier artifact requires revision on the supplied evidence; all field evidence remains pending.","objections":["Hypothetical learner dissent: “Showing my disagreement identifies me.” Offer contribution without screen entry; paper also has visibility limits.","Hypothetical teacher dissent: paper-only discussion may preserve more reasoning time than device management.","Unknowns: reading and language burden, device settings, discussion norms, support capacity and acceptable workload."],"prototype_html":"","revisit_stage":"","risks":["Rehearsal stage progression could be mistaken for classroom authorization.","Copied prompts, fluent previews or representation labels could become assessment proxies.","Screenshots, browser history or external notes could retain identifiable wording."],"system_capabilities":["Display invented evidence and optional prompts; produce requested literal previews without grades, rankings or inferred capability.","Support deliberate replacement, cancellation, recovery and preview invalidation; clear application-owned copies at handoff.","Keep participant records, telemetry, storage and network calls absent; keep external synthetic checks separate from learner inquiry."],"tradeoffs":["Recovery protects qualifications but adds visible copies and controls.","Minimal collection protects dignity but limits retrospective inquiry.","Structured prompts support discussion but can supply apparent reasoning."]},"seq":51,"stage":"deploy"},{"agent":"measurement","response":{"advantages":["An evidence ledger makes unsupported conclusions and missing observations visible.","Separate reasoning, access and workload judgments preserve task-specific interpretation.","Paper comparison can examine whether browser controls justify their additional demands."],"artifact_body":"Status and basis
This is a Measurement expert draft, not an approved evaluation requirement or inquiry protocol. Sequence 37 supplies an unexecuted candidate; 42 proposes comparison and coverage safeguards; 44 integrates separate evidence streams; 51 preserves revision-specific human gates. No technical observations, participant evidence, approvals, measured results or demonstrated proficiency exist. Field evidence remains pending. The present evaluation conclusion is that performance and classroom suitability are unestablished, not that the candidate succeeded or failed.

Challenge and proposed Evaluate change
Before: 51 carries a review packet and learning cautions forward without specifying how Evaluate must adjudicate each proposed conclusion.
After: require an external claim ledger identifying the exact claim, relevant evidence stream, candidate revision, observation, source of wording, assistance, rival explanation, missing coverage and permissible conclusion. Distinguish absent evidence from observed difficulty and from behavior not elicited. No aggregate success judgment, score or proficiency label follows. This adds an Evaluate requirement; no supplied finding invalidates an earlier artifact.

Blank claim ledger
Claim under review: __. Candidate/task/context: __. Evidence needed: __. Observed behavior: pending. Evidence location: pending. Learner explanation versus supplied text: unresolved. Explanatory hints and access supports: __. Rival explanation: __. Missing observations or coverage: __. Narrow conclusion: unestablished. Unresolved dissent: __. Next review action: __.
Use fictional inputs for technical review. Any later participant record requires the separately reviewed purpose, minimum information, access, retention, correction and reuse arrangements in 51. Do not turn the synthetic worksheet into a learner log.

Evidence boundaries
Technical: outcomes from 44 remain untested. A future observed transition supports only that behavior in the tested revision, browser and input conditions. It cannot establish secure erasure, accessibility across settings or learning.
Reasoning: seek an explanation connecting a claim to an observation, comparing recorded conditions, distinguishing final height from increase and explaining causal uncertainty. Keep fixed notices and copied hints distinguishable from explanations. Retaining, narrowing, revising or withholding can each be reasoned. Editable initial/current text cannot establish improvement; group wording cannot establish individual capability. Blank text means text not supplied.
Participation and workload: examine opportunities to inspect evidence, contribute, correct recorder wording and dissent, alongside preparation, facilitation and recovery effort. Completion and silence do not establish access, agreement or acceptable workload.

Comparison and causal threats
Teacher-facilitated paper discussion using the labelled A/B/C cards and matching optional prompts is the complete non-AI option. Ask whether the browser contributes useful correction or evidence-inspection opportunities relative to its reading and management demands. Match evidence, time, prompts and supports where practicable; report actual differences.
Self-selected routes cannot identify a device effect. A later reviewed comparison could use distinct matched tasks and counterbalanced order, while documenting assistance and implementation differences. Carryover, prior familiarity, recorder influence, novelty, task difficulty and facilitator prompting remain rival explanations. Plant observations themselves cannot isolate water or light effects: starting height and other conditions are missing.

Transfer, coverage and unknowns
A fresh fictional task should change the evidence structure, rather than repeat the plant story's counterexample pattern. An open prompt with evidence and language supports available can examine reasoning in that task; immediate performance cannot establish retention. A separately reviewed delayed task is needed for any retention claim. Neither establishes broad transfer without further evidence.
Review coverage across paper/shared-device routes, reading and language presentation, oral/drawing/written contributions and discussion arrangements. Mark planned, observed and unknown coverage separately. No coverage is observed here. Do not infer disability or ability from route choice. If a relevant context is absent, withhold conclusions about it rather than treating missing complaints as equitable access.

Decision and human gates
Next, prepare the blank ledger alongside 44's synthetic worksheet and 51's decision record for review. Pending technical execution, human interaction testing and teacher, student and caregiver review must remain explicit. Any real inquiry requires separate human review; classroom use requires a revision-specific human decision. Preserve dissent that paper alone may better fit available discussion time. No pilot, launch, efficacy, equity or proficiency claim is supported by this rehearsal.","artifact_title":"Evaluate expert draft: evidence ledger and limits on conclusions","challenged_assumption":"A bounded review plan is sufficient to support evaluation conclusions without explicit claim-level evidence, missingness and coverage rules.","decision":"Continue Evaluate as a rehearsal by proposing the claim ledger and explicit missing-evidence rules for review. Keep technical execution, human gates and field inquiry pending; request no earlier-stage revision without an established invalidating finding.","drawbacks":["Separate evidence review adds preparation and interpretation effort.","Optional participation and minimal collection limit individual and subgroup conclusions.","Fresh tasks introduce language and task-comparability uncertainties."],"evidence_refs":[37,42,44,51],"flourishing":["Agency and dignity: passing, withholding and declining recording remain legitimate, not adverse outcomes.","Access and belonging: examine opportunities to inspect, contribute, correct and dissent across participation routes.","Sustainable workload and learning/transfer: examine teacher effort separately from task reasoning, retention and transfer."],"human_capabilities":["Reviewers must distinguish observation, interpretation, rival explanation and missing evidence.","Facilitators must preserve language supports, optional contribution, correction and dissent.","An accountable human owner and teacher, student and caregiver reviewers must examine proposed use and any separate inquiry before real participation."],"message":"Sequence 51 preserves human gates, but Evaluate also needs explicit rules for withholding conclusions when checks, comparison evidence or participation coverage are absent. Sequences 37 and 44 supply a candidate and protocol, not findings. I propose a claim-by-claim evidence ledger that separates untested behavior, task reasoning, access, workload and transfer. This is a Measurement expert draft for the rehearsal. No checks, participant observations, approvals or measured results exist; field evidence remains pending.","objections":["Hypothetical teacher dissent: “Paper cards support this discussion without administering another evaluation process.” Preserve paper-only delivery.","Rival explanations: fluent wording may reflect copying or recorder assistance; sparse wording may reflect reading, language, typing or discussion pressure.","Unknowns: task comparability, classroom languages, device access, discussion norms, coverage and acceptable workload."],"prototype_html":"","revisit_stage":"","risks":["An empty results record could be interpreted as evidence of no problems.","Copied limitations or group wording could be attributed to individual understanding.","Route selection, assistance and repeated exposure could produce misleading comparisons."],"system_capabilities":["Keep optional discussion wording separate from external evaluation records; collect no participant telemetry.","Expose invented evidence, supplied hints and correction without automatic reasoning judgments.","Preserve the paper route and synthetic interaction checks without learner identities, grades or rankings."],"tradeoffs":["Less explanatory cueing improves interpretation but can increase task demands.","Minimal collection protects dignity while limiting coverage claims.","Matching facilitation aids comparison but may obscure differences in ordinary workload."]},"seq":57,"stage":"evaluate"},{"agent":"orchestrator","response":{"advantages":["Claim-specific records distinguish documented design changes from tested behavior and learning.","Correction, optional recording and preserved dissent support learner choice.","A complete paper option permits review of whether browser controls justify their demands."],"artifact_body":"Status and evaluation conclusion
This is an integrated rehearsal evaluation artifact, not an approved inquiry protocol or classroom authorization. Sequence 37 provides an unexecuted candidate; 44 provides separate evidence streams; 51 supplies revision-specific human gates; 57 proposes claim-level adjudication. No technical checks, participant observations, measured results, approvals or demonstrated proficiency exist. Field evidence remains pending. Performance and suitability are unestablished; this is neither a success nor a failure finding.

Adopted Evaluate requirement
Every proposed conclusion must identify its claim, candidate revision/task/context, evidence stream, observation and evidence location, supplied versus learner wording, assistance, rival explanation, missing coverage, permissible conclusion, dissent and next action. Keep absent evidence, observed difficulty and behavior not elicited distinct. An empty record means evidence is absent. No aggregate success judgment, score, ranking or proficiency label follows.

Current claim ledger
Specification response: hypothetical sequence 27 exposed unspecified occupied-field insertion; Design 29 added deliberate replacement and recovery, carried into Build 37. Supplied artifacts document a meaningful revision. They do not establish control comprehension, successful execution or learner benefit. Next: inspect and test the exact candidate using fictional wording.
Technical behavior: replacement, cancellation, independent recovery, preview invalidation, literal rendering, reveal and handoff are specified in 37 and 44. Observations and evidence locations: pending; outcome: untested. Browser/version, viewport and input coverage: unknown. Next: execute the external synthetic worksheet. Any later result supports only the tested behavior and conditions; it cannot establish secure erasure or general accessibility.
Reasoning: proposed evidence connects claims to observations, compares recorded conditions, distinguishes final height from increase and explains causal uncertainty. Observations: absent. Supplied limitations and hints are scaffolding; editable initial/current entries cannot authenticate improvement, and group wording cannot establish individual capability. Conclusion: reasoning, retention and transfer are unestablished.
Participation and workload: opportunities to inspect, contribute, correct and dissent, plus preparation, facilitation and recovery effort, are proposed review targets. Coverage and observations: absent. Silence and completion establish neither belonging nor acceptable burden. Classroom suitability and route equivalence remain unknown.

Reusable record
