view."],"message":"Evaluate integrates the candidate (37), evidence protocol (44), human decision gates (51) and claim-ledger proposal (57). The record supports traceable specification changes, including replacement safeguards after the hypothetical challenge (27, 29); it supplies no tested behavior or learning outcomes. Adopt the evaluation artifact below. Technical performance, classroom suitability, access, workload, retention and transfer remain unestablished. Field evidence and all human approvals remain pending. No supplied finding requires an earlier-stage revision.","objections":["Hypothetical teacher dissent: paper cards may leave more discussion time than browser management and evaluation administration.","Hypothetical learner dissent: displaying disagreement may identify its author even without names; participation without screen entry must remain available.","Unknowns include reading and language burden, device access, discussion norms, task comparability, coverage and acceptable workload."],"prototype_html":"","revisit_stage":"","risks":["Untested behavior or missing complaints could be reported as successful performance or equitable access.","Copied limitations and recorder fluency could be mistaken for learner reasoning.","Stage progression or review completion could be mistaken for classroom authorization."],"system_capabilities":["Display invented evidence and limitations; provide optional editable wording and requested literal previews without consequential judgments.","Support deliberate replacement, cancellation, recovery and handoff clearing; these behaviors remain untested.","Keep participant records, telemetry, storage and network calls absent; maintain external synthetic review separately from learner inquiry."],"tradeoffs":["Prompts support explanation but can supply the reasoning being examined.","Recovery preserves qualifications while adding visible copies and reading demands.","Minimal collection protects dignity while limiting individual and coverage conclusions."]},"seq":59,"stage":"evaluate"},{"agent":"scenario","response":{"advantages":["Preserves language support without overstating independent reasoning.","Makes ambiguous assistance visible in evaluation.","Applies equally to browser and paper discussion."],"artifact_body":"Status and basis
This is one hypothetical event in the rehearsal, not an observed difficulty, approved requirement or empirical finding. Sequence 37 supplies the unexecuted plant-evidence candidate. Instrument 44 distinguishes explanatory hints from language assistance; Evaluate 59 requires source-of-wording and assistance records. Field evidence, technical execution and all human approvals remain pending.

Hypothetical event
During a future discussion of A/B/C, a facilitator translates the missing-starting-height notice and adds: “So B being taller does not show that it grew more.” A learner then uses that sentence in an explanation. Suppose an external reviewer records only “language support” and treats the final-height-versus-increase distinction as learner-generated reasoning. A hypothetical teacher objects: “My translation included the inference you are trying to examine.” This does not establish that the learner lacks understanding; it exposes ambiguous attribution.

Challenged assumption
Recording assistance under a language-support label sufficiently distinguishes access assistance from supplied scientific reasoning. Instrument is the earliest affected artifact because it defines the prospective evidence record and support distinctions; the fictional evidence and browser interaction need no change for this scenario.

Requested requirement change
Before: 44 records explanatory hints and language/access support without an explicit rule for assistance that performs both functions.
After: revise the external Instrument record to permit access support, explanatory support, mixed support and support content unknown. Classify what assistance supplied, rather than classifying the learner or inferring disability. When assistance supplies the reasoning relation under review, the permissible conclusion is that the explanation occurred with that relation supplied; independent production remains unestablished. When content is unknown, withhold source attribution. Do not treat either category as evidence of inability.
Prepare fictional examples for reviewer discussion, including a translation that preserves wording and one that adds an inference. These examples are drafts requiring human review, not verified translations. Keep support available during any later reviewed task. Any further invitation to explain using another comparison is optional, remains supported, and cannot automatically establish independence or transfer.
Use the existing synthetic worksheet to rehearse classification with invented facilitator wording. Do not add browser telemetry or create participant records. Any later collection of assistance details remains subject to 51's separate purpose, minimum-information, access, retention, correction and reuse gates.

Non-AI option and dissent
Teacher-facilitated paper discussion remains complete: labelled A/B/C cards, optional claim and uncertainty spaces, oral explanation, drawing and correctable supported recording. Apply the same attribution limits to paper. Preserve the hypothetical teacher objection that recording support distinctions may consume discussion time; a legitimate choice is to facilitate without collecting individual reasoning evidence.

Rivals and unknowns
The learner might already understand the inference, imitate unfamiliar wording, or adopt a newly supplied explanation. The hypothetical sentence cannot distinguish these possibilities. Languages, support practices, reading burden and acceptable review effort remain unknown.

Next action
Request one Instrument revision adding mixed/unknown assistance and bounded attribution rules. Carry those rules into Evaluate's ledger. Rehearse them with fictional examples before considering separately reviewed human inquiry. No launch, pilot, efficacy, access or learning claim follows.","artifact_title":"Scenario draft: translation supplies the inference under review","challenged_assumption":"A language-support label sufficiently distinguishes access assistance from scientific reasoning supplied by a facilitator.","decision":"Request an Instrument revision specifying mixed and unknown assistance, preserving support and withholding unsupported attribution; then apply the revised rules to Evaluate.","drawbacks":["Support classification adds reviewer effort.","Some reasoning conclusions must remain unresolved.","Prepared examples require language and teacher review."],"evidence_refs":[37,44,51,59],"flourishing":["Agency and dignity: learners may accept support, correct wording and decline recording.","Access and belonging: mixed support must never justify exclusion or inferred disability.","Sustainable workload and learning/transfer: use brief review categories; supported performance does not establish independence or transfer."],"human_capabilities":["Facilitators must maintain language access without treating support as failure.","Reviewers must recognize mixed assistance and withhold unsupported attribution.","Teacher, student and caregiver reviewers must examine proposed support examples before real use."],"message":"Hypothetical challenge: a facilitator's translation also supplies a scientific inference, but a reviewer labels it only as language support. Sequences 44 and 59 require distinguishing assistance from learner explanation without specifying how to handle mixed support. Request an Instrument revision that preserves access support while withholding unsupported attribution. This is a scenario draft; no interaction or participant observation occurred.","objections":["Hypothetical teacher dissent: separating translation from explanation during discussion may be impractical.","Rival explanation: a learner may already understand the inference but use the facilitator's wording.","Unknowns: classroom languages, support practices and reviewer agreement remain unexamined."],"prototype_html":"","revisit_stage":"instrument","risks":["A supplied inference could be credited as learner reasoning.","Reviewers could withdraw needed support to obtain cleaner evidence.","Detailed assistance records could become unauthorized learner histories."],"system_capabilities":["Keep external review categories separate from optional browser wording.","Add no learner tracking, support scores or automatic assistance classification.","Retain fictional evidence, correction and the complete paper route."],"tradeoffs":["Accessible explanation versus attribution of reasoning to the learner.","Useful support descriptions versus minimal collection.","Consistent review categories versus context-sensitive interpretation."]},"seq":62,"stage":"evaluate"},{"agent":"orchestrator","response":{"advantages":["Mixed and unknown assistance become explicit without judging learners.","Claim-specific attribution preserves language access and limits unsupported conclusions.","The same evidence boundaries apply to browser and paper discussion."],"artifact_body":"Status and event
This is an integrated Evaluate artifact for the lifecycle rehearsal, not an approved inquiry protocol or classroom authorization. Sequence 62 hypothesizes a facilitator translating the missing-starting-height notice while adding the inference that greater final height does not establish greater growth. A reviewer then attributes that relation to the learner under a language-support label. No such interaction occurred. Build 37 remains unexecuted; technical checks, participant observations, measured results, human approvals and field evidence remain pending.

Decision and earliest revision
Request one targeted revision of Instrument 44. Its prospective records distinguish explanatory hints and language/access support but leave assistance serving both functions unspecified. Add mixed and unknown support content and a claim-specific attribution rule. Instrument is the earliest affected artifact because the gap concerns evidence recording and interpretation. The supplied event does not invalidate the fictional evidence or interaction specification. Carry the rule into Evaluate 59 now; the requested Instrument revision remains outstanding.

Replacement requirement for Instrument 44
For each reasoning claim, identify the particular relation being examined, such as final height versus growth increase. Describe assistance relative to that relation using access support, explanatory support, mixed support or support content unknown. Record what was supplied when known; a label such as translation cannot establish its reasoning contribution. Access support describes assistance making the evidence or wording available without supplying the target relation; explanatory support supplies that relation; mixed support does both. These are descriptions of assistance, never learner classifications or verified translations.
When the target relation was supplied, conclude only that the explanation occurred with that relation supplied. Independent production remains unestablished. When assistance content is unknown, withhold source attribution. Neither case establishes inability or lack of understanding. Do not infer absent assistance from an empty record. Keep support available; optional further explanation remains supported task evidence and cannot automatically establish independence or transfer.

Usable external record
Claim/relation: __. Candidate revision/task/context: __. Evidence stream: __. Supplied notices or hints: __. Assistance content and basis for description: pending. Support description relative to target: unknown. Learner contribution distinguishable from supplied content: unresolved. Observation and evidence location: pending. Rival explanation: __. Missing coverage: __. Permissible conclusion: unestablished. Dissent/correction: __. Next action: __.
Use invented wording for rehearsal. Any later participant collection must first satisfy 51's separately reviewed purpose, minimum-information, visibility, access, retention, correction and reuse arrangements. Do not silently convert the synthetic worksheet into a learner log.

Fictional classification exercises
These are invented paraphrase examples, not translations, observations or reviewer-agreement results. For the target relation final height versus growth increase: repeating “Starting heights are unrecorded” does not itself state that relation, although it supplies relevant information. Adding “Therefore B's final height does not show that it grew more” supplies the relation; if also providing language access, the expected draft description is mixed support. An account saying only “translation provided” leaves content unknown and cannot support source attribution. Classification must change with the target claim: the first example already supplies the missing-starting-height fact. Rehearse these distinctions outside the candidate; outcomes remain pending.

Evaluation boundaries and non-AI option
Preserve 57 and 59's separate technical, reasoning, participation and workload conclusions. Completion, copied limitations, editable initial/current wording and group previews establish neither individual improvement nor agreement. Existing understanding, imitation and newly adopted explanation remain rival explanations. Plant differences may involve water, light, starting height or missing conditions.
