covery and external review work. Build 37 adds correction controls; Deploy 51 requires human gates; Instrument 67 expands claim-specific documentation. These specifications do not establish that one facilitator can manage all three while preserving participation. I propose separate facilitation and inquiry modes, prepared recovery materials and revision-linked handoff requirements. This is an expert rehearsal draft; field evidence remains pending.","objections":["Hypothetical teacher dissent: “I can facilitate the discussion or document each assistance episode, but cannot reliably do both.”","Rivals: missing dissent may reflect agreement, recorder influence, time pressure or an unavailable contribution route.","Unknowns: staffing, reading and language burden, device reliability, discussion norms and acceptable preparation effort."],"prototype_html":"","revisit_stage":"","risks":["Documentation could displace correction, language support or dissent.","Outdated paper or review materials could contradict the candidate.","Device failure could erase wording and pressure learners to reconstruct it."],"system_capabilities":["Keep optional discussion wording separate from external inquiry records and consequential judgments.","Preserve cancellation and correction; disclose refresh loss and bounded handoff clearing.","Maintain revision-linked evidence cards, paper prompts, support inventory and review instructions."],"tradeoffs":["Detailed attribution records versus attention available for discussion.","Recoverable wording versus visible copies on a shared device.","Consistent instructions versus adaptation to language and participation needs."]},"seq":73,"stage":"refine"},{"agent":"learning","response":{"advantages":["Descriptive feedback connects practice to a specific reasoning action.","Optional retries preserve support, correction and learner choice.","Different evidence structures help expose reliance on the counterexample pattern."],"artifact_body":"Status and basis
This is a Learning scientist expert draft for the rehearsal, not an approved requirement or inquiry protocol. Build 37 remains unexecuted. Deploy 51 retains human gates. Evaluate 64 and Instrument 67 bound assistance attribution; Systems 73 separates facilitation from inquiry. No participant observations, human approvals, measured results or demonstrated proficiency exist. Field evidence remains pending. Completion is not competence.

Challenge and proposed Refine change
Before: Build offers another-pair practice and the review packet cautions against learning claims, but the feedback-and-retry routine remains unspecified.
After: propose an optional human-facilitated cycle: choose one reasoning action, invite an explanation, describe the specific contribution, offer one next question, and invite another attempt or a decision to stop. Treat this as a practice arrangement, never a learner classification. Do not require changed wording, agreement or a completed field. This adds a Refine proposal; no supplied finding requires reopening an earlier stage.

Usable facilitation routine
Choose a target such as connecting a claim to an observation, comparing recorded conditions, or explaining a limitation. Ask the learner which comparison they want to discuss. Keep cards, language support and earlier prompts available.
Describe only what the learner actually offered. A conditional template is: “You compared __ and __ and pointed to __. Your explanation connects that observation to __. What remains uncertain?” If that connection was not offered, omit it. Do not praise response length or announce competence. Invite correction: “Does that describe what you meant?”
Offer one focused next question. For example, after an A/C comparison: “Which recorded conditions match, which differ, and what is unrecorded?” This prompt itself supplies a comparison structure; apply 67's claim-specific support rules if a separately reviewed inquiry records the episode. A facilitator who supplies a scientific inference must identify it as supplied rather than credit it as independently produced.
Invite optional retry using B/C, or permit the learner to revisit the same pair, retain a qualified claim, withhold judgment or stop. Ask what observation now supports the wording and why. Another pair within the plant activity is supported practice, not transfer. Repeating a supplied sentence does not establish understanding; explaining its application may provide supported task evidence if appropriately reviewed.

Practice card for rehearsal
Target reasoning action: __. Fictional comparison: __. Open invitation: __. Conditional feedback wording: __. Reasoning supplied by the prompt or feedback: __. Optional next attempt: __. Correction or dissent route: __. Unresolved access/workload question: __.
Prepare this with invented responses only. In facilitation mode, use it as a planning aid without individual records. Under 73, pause inquiry recording if it competes with support, correction or discussion. Missing records limit conclusions; they never justify withdrawing support.

Fresh-task draft for later review
Use an entirely invented paper-bridge story: P has 1 layer, span 10 story units and reported load 3 story units; Q has 2 layers, span 15 and load 5; R has 3 layers, span 20 and load 7. All numbers are fictional inputs, not experimental results or construction guidance. Other conditions are unrecorded. Proposed claim: “Adding layers caused the larger reported load.” Open invitation: “What can these observations support, and what remains uncertain?”
Unlike the plant counterexample, the reported load increases throughout; every pair also changes span. The draft therefore invites scrutiny of causal attribution without a direct counterexample. It requires human review of wording, interpretation and access before use. It is a candidate task, not a validated transfer measure. Keep support available and describe supplied content relative to the exact relation examined. Performance would concern this task only.

Retention and non-AI option
A separate task after a reviewed delay would be needed to investigate retention; specify interval, intervening exposure and supports before any inquiry. No interval, task equivalence or retention result is established here.
The complete non-AI option is teacher-facilitated paper discussion with labelled invented A/B/C cards, C held until requested, separate suggestion slips and optional explanation spaces. Use the same feedback routine through speech, drawing, pointing with explanation or correctable supported recording. Paper visibility and route equivalence remain unknown.

