Prepare fictional feedback examples and the fresh-task card for human review alongside 67's attribution examples and 73's workload walkthrough. Preserve dissent that paper discussion without individual evidence collection may be preferable. Teacher, student and caregiver review, interaction testing and revision-specific human decisions remain pending. Any participant inquiry requires separate review under 51. Withhold efficacy, independent capability, retention and transfer conclusions.","artifact_title":"Refine learning draft: descriptive feedback, purposeful retries and fresh-task boundaries","challenged_assumption":"Offering another comparison and editable reconsideration is sufficient to provide purposeful practice or evidence of learning without specifying feedback, supplied reasoning and distinct later tasks.","decision":"Propose the optional feedback-and-retry routine and fresh-task draft for Refine review. Next prepare fictional examples alongside sequences 67 and 73; keep execution, human gates and field inquiry pending. Request no earlier-stage revision.","drawbacks":["Feedback and task preparation require facilitator attention.","Supported explanations cannot establish independent capability.","Fresh tasks introduce language and comparability uncertainties."],"evidence_refs":[37,51,64,67,73],"flourishing":["Agency and dignity: learners may retain, withhold, pass or correct facilitator wording.","Access and belonging: preserve language support, oral explanation, drawing and paper participation.","Sustainable workload and learning/transfer: make practice optional and separate facilitation from evidence collection."],"human_capabilities":["Facilitators must describe a specific contribution, invite explanation and make their wording correctable.","Reviewers must distinguish supplied reasoning, supported application, retention and transfer evidence.","Human reviewers must examine task wording, access, workload and inquiry boundaries before real use."],"message":"Refine should specify how supported discussion becomes purposeful practice without becoming an assessment proxy. Sequence 73 separates facilitation from inquiry; sequence 67 bounds attribution of supplied reasoning. Neither yet specifies a descriptive feedback-and-retry cycle. I propose an optional human-facilitated practice routine and distinct retention/transfer drafts. This is an expert contribution to a rehearsal, not an approved requirement. No checks, participant observations, approvals or learning results exist; field evidence remains pending.","objections":["Hypothetical teacher dissent: another feedback routine may consume the discussion time it aims to improve.","Rivals: fluent reconsideration may reflect copying, prior understanding, recorder influence or supplied inference.","Unknowns: reading burden, classroom languages, discussion norms, task difficulty and acceptable effort."],"prototype_html":"","revisit_stage":"","risks":["A revised sentence could be mistaken for improved understanding.","Feedback could become answer substitution or pressure to revise.","Transfer testing could displace access support or ordinary discussion."],"system_capabilities":["Keep evidence and optional prompts available during practice; provide no automatic capability judgments.","Preserve editable wording, cancellation and dissent without requiring revision or browser entry.","Keep feedback and inquiry records outside the candidate; add no tracking or support classification."],"tradeoffs":["Specific feedback supports practice while potentially supplying reasoning.","Repeated practice supports reconsideration but increases familiarity.","Minimal recording protects dignity while limiting learning conclusions."]},"seq":75,"stage":"refine"}]
